2 TITL Origin Displayed: English - Origins Available: English, German. Spelling variations of this family name include: Fisher, Fischer and others. First found in Warwickshire where they were seated from very early times and were granted lands by Duke Wil
3 CONC liam of Normandy, their liege Lord, for their distinguished assistance at the Battle of Hastings in 1066 C.E. Some of the first settlers of this family name or some of its variants were: Edward Fisher who settled in Virginia in 1623; Gabriel Fishe
3 CONC r settled in Virginia in 1635; Henry Fisher settled in Virginia in 1623 with his wife and children. (From www.HouseOfNames.com Archives copyright  2000 - 2009). Motto Translated: Regard the end. This interesting surname has a number of origins. Fir
3 CONC st, it may be an occupational name for a fisherman, deriving from the Middle English "fisher", a development of the Old English pre-7th Century "fiscere", a derivative of "fiscian" meaning "to catch fish". Second, it may be a topographical name fo
3 CONC r someone who lived near a fish weir on a river, deriving from the Middle English "fisch", Old English "fisc" meaning "fish" plus the Middle English "gere" a development of the Old Norse "gervi" "weir, apparatus". Finally, it may be an Ashkenazic o
3 CONC ccupational name for a fisherman from the Yiddish "fisher". The surname dates back to the mid-13th Century. London Church Records include the marriage of Annis Fisher to Robart Marle on July 6th 1549, at St. Martin Orgar, and the christening of Ann
3 CONC e, daughter of Thomas Fisher, on 8 March 1552 at St. Mary Magdalene's, Bermondsey. The name is well represented in the "Dictionary of National Biography" with over twenty entries, one of the most notable being George Fisher (1794 - 1873), who acte
3 CONC d as astronomer to the Polar expedition of 1818, and propounded a theory of the nature and origin of the aurora borealis, published in 1834. A Coat of Arms granted to a Fisher family in Scotland is a blue shield, with a silver chevron between thre
3 CONC e silver salmon haurient. The first recorded spelling of the family name is shown to be that of Richard le Fischer, which was dated 1263 in the "Feet of Fines of Essex", during the reign of King Henry 3rd, known as "The Frenchman", 1216 - 1272. Sur
3 CONC names became necessary when governments introduced personal taxation. In England this was known as Poll Tax. Throughout the centuries, surnames in every country have continued to "develop" often leading to astonishing variants of the original spell
3 CONC ing.  Copyright: Name Origin Research www.surnamedb.com 1980 - 2014.
