2 NOTE @N2732@
1 _MILT Member of the Sandwich militia August 1643.  On 6 MAR 1654/5 he was made ensign of the Eastham Company. Became a Lieutenant before 6 OCT 1659.  He was in the Indian Wars against King Philip beginning in 1671, starting as 2nd in command as a Lt., t
2 CONC hen a Captain, eventually becoming a Major of the Cape Company known as the 3rd Regiment on 2 JUN 1685.
1 EVEN
2 TYPE bio
2 NOTE Description: (1) VIEW BIOGRAPHICAL ITEMS IN STORIES (MEMORIES) FOR THIS RECORD, LZD7-W4H.  See biographical & family data in sketch for Edmond Freeman in outstanding historical series, "The Great Migration....," compiled by Robert Charles Anderson
3 CONC , George F. Sanborn, Jr., & Melinde Lutz Sanborn (2001). (2) See also BIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH [CONTRIBUTED ON 5 January 2015 by LaRita Call Evans1] Both Edmond and his brother William, with their respective wives, were beneficiaries in the wills of the
3 CONC ir father-in-law John Hodsoll and his son of the same name. The wives were co-heirs to their brother. In January 1633/34, Edmond, on behalf of himself and his five living children, brought suit in Chancery Court against his brother William and othe
3 CONC rs in regard to the disposition of certain lands and tenements formerly belonging to John Hodsoll, his father-in-law. The depositions taken give evidence of the dates of baptism of four of the six children at Billingshurst, and that he owned land
3 CONC s in both Pulborough and Billingshurst. He moved to Billingshurst, about 6 miles NE of Pulborough about 1619. By September 1627 he had returned to Pulborough, where his youngest child was baptized and buried.  Edmond emigrated to America on the shi
3 CONC p "Abigail", arriving at Boston 8 Oct 1635, with wife Elizabeth and children Edmond, John, Elizabeth and Alice. They first settled at Saugus (Lynn), then at Sandwich in 1637. Assistant in 1640, and reelected many years. Freeman in Plymouth 2 Jan 16
3 CONC 37. His will was dated 21 June 1682, and probated 2 Nov 1682. Amerian Geneologes 
2 NOTE @N2734@
