3 CONT "Genealogy of the French Family," (Camden, ME: Camden Publishing Company, 1903).
3 CONT 
3 CONT Lieut. WILLIAM FRENCH (or Frenche) who was our earliest ancestor in the direct line in America, was born in Halsted, Essex Co., England, March 15, 1603. He married Elizabeth ----, and had four children born in England. He came to America with Rev
3 CONC . Thomas Shepard and his brother Samuel, Roger Harlaksuden, and George and Joseph Cook, in the ship "Defence" in the summer of 1635. He settled first in Cambridge, Mass, and in 1652 was one of the original proprietors and earliest settlers of Bille
3 CONC rica, Mass. He was a Lieutenant of the militia and afterwards Captain. Was chosen to "sit in the Deacons seat" 1659. "Comitioner to establish the county rates" the same year; one of the first selectmen, 1660, and served nine years. Committee to exa
3 CONC mine children and servants in "reading, religion, and the catechism," 1651; the first Deputy or Representative of Billerica in the General Court at Boston, 1660, taking his seat in 1663. Evidence of his activity in the cause of Indian instruction i
3 CONC s found in a letter written by him to "a godly friend in England," published in London in the famous tract, "Strength Out of Weakness," and afterwards republished in the Mass. Hist. Soc. Coll 3d S. vol. 4, pp. 149, 196, in which he gives a detaile
3 CONC d account of the testimony of an Indian convert. His wife died March 31, 1668, and he married 2d, Mary, daughter of Thomas Lathrop of Barnstable and widow of John Stearns of Billerica. He died Nov. 20, 1681, and his widow afterwards married Isaac M
3 CONC ixter of Watertown as his third wife.
