0 @N1023@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Birth: ABT 1642 Stamford, Fairfield Co, Connecticut
1 CONT 
1 CONT Illness: 1676
1 CONT Details: Seriously ill from an unspecified sickness in the summer of 1676, he recovered, although his brother John Newman and their father William Newman died that same year.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Occupation: A farmer, he owned serially seven home lots in central Stamford in addition to many properties in the outlying areas.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Land: During his bachelorhood and early married years, his first house and 1.25-acre home lot were situated on the north side of the Country Road in Stamford, at the present-day junction of West Park Place and Main Street, now transected from nort
1 CONC h to south by Summer Street (41.053686, -73.540792). The property was willed to him by his father on 18 August 1676 and sold by Thomas Newman on 4 January 1681/82.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birth of Son: 1680
1 CONT Name: John Newman
1 CONT 
1 CONT Land: On 4 January 1681/82, Thomas Newman purchased a quarter-acre home lot opposite the Stamford Burying Ground and his grandfather’s former home lot (41.053116, -73.543017). This property was sold on 1 March 1686/87.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birth of Daughter: 1686
1 CONT Name: Elizabeth Newman
1 CONT 
1 CONT Land: On 4 March 1686/87, Thomas Newman purchased a 1.5-acre home lot on the north side of East Street, later called Main Street, but now covered by Stamford Town Center (41.054279, -73.536502). This property was sold on 16 February 1687/88.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Land: On 16 February 1687/88, Thomas Newman purchased a 1.5-acre home lot west of the Rippowam River (Mill River) on the south side of the Country Road, later called West Broad Street (41.055249, -73.548079). This property was sold on 7 October 16
1 CONC 91.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birth of Son: 1688
1 CONT Name: Nathaniel Newman
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birth of Son: 1690
1 CONT Name: Jonathan Newman
1 CONT 
1 CONT Land: On 7 October 1691, Thomas Newman purchased a 3-acre home lot on the west side of West Street, later known as Washington Boulevard, opposite the home lot of John Smith (and formerly the home lot of Henry Smith), now partially covered by Tress
1 CONC er Boulevard (41.051299, -73.543011). This property was sold on 18 May 1696.
