0 @N1361@ NOTE https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/10943434/samuel-man
0 @N1362@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT The couple married in Dedham and there are notes indicating maybe Nathanell and possibly Samuell were born in Dedham but nothing for Mary in Dedham. Unclear where they lived when first married. No record either son William.	
1 CONT Preacher in Milford & Wrentham.Taught in Dedham for 5 years
1 CONT 
1 CONT According to "Wrentham: A History" by Jordan D. Fiore, Samuel was hired on 13 May 1667 to be the first minister of Wrentham (Wollomonopoag).  Samuel, a native of Cambridge, had graduated from Harvard in 1665, and was hired for the sum of L120 per y
1 CONC ear, to be paid semi-annually.  Samuel was also the schoolmaster until June of 1672.  By February 1673 the town had voted to "lay out a house lott for Man at Wollomonopoag", allot him ten cow-commons, first choice for one-half of his meadowland, an
1 CONC d fifty pounds for construction of his house, all in addition to his promised salary.  It was probably on the basis of this promise that Samuel was able to marry Esther.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Samuel was known as not only a good man, but a great and learned man. He apparently performed a great many services for the residents of Wrentham over and above his Sunday ministrations.  He and Esther raised a large and good family, active in th
1 CONC e affairs of Wrentham and nearby towns for nearly two centuries.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Samuel'sbirth year calculated from his date of hire 13 May 1667, as Wrentham's first minister.  According to "Wrentham: A History", he was twenty-two years old at that time.
1 CONT 
1 CONT A volume of Wrentham Vital Records reports a monument in the Wrentham Cemetery to four ministers of the Congregational Church shows Samuel's death day as the 23rd and died at age 72 years.  He was a minister for 49 years.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Inscription on grave marker: "Here lyes the body of Rev'd Mr Samuel Mann who dec'd May the 22nd 1719 in the 72 year of his age" (from Churches of Dedham, Massachusetts (1638-1844) by Robert Brand Hanson, p. 139
1 CONT 
1 CONT "brother Mr. Robert Ware and son Pelatiah " named as executors of his estate.
