0 @N1440@ NOTE 5. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Issue of John Freeman, who m. Mercy Prence. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 24. I. John, Feb. 2, 1650; d. inft. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT *25. II. John, Dec. 1651, in Eastham; m. Sarah Merick, dr. of Wm., Dec. 18, 1672, and 2d, "Mrs. Mercy Watson, wid. of Elkanah of Plym.," 1701.
1 CONT  
1 CONT *26. III. Thomas, Sept. 1653; m. Rebecca Sparrow, dr. of Jona., Esq., Dec. 31, 1673. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT *27. IV. Patience; m. Lt. Samuel Paine, Jan. 31, 1682-83.
1 CONT  
1 CONT *28. V. Hannah; m. John Mayo, April 14, 1681. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT *29. VI. Edmund, June, 1657; m. prob. Ruth Merrick, dr. of Wm. ; and 2d, Sarah Mayo, dr. of Samuel. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT *30. VII. Mercy, July, 1659; m. Samuel Knowles, Dec. 1679.
1 CONT  
1 CONT *31. VIII. William, b. abt. 1660; m. Lydia Sparrow, dr. of Jno., abt. 1684-5. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 32. IX. Prince, Feb. 3, 1665-6; d. young. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT *33. X. Nathaniel, March 20, 1669, "youngest son;" m. Mary, abt. 1690.
1 CONT  
1 CONT *34. XI. Bennet, March 7, 1670-1 ; m. Dea. John Paine of E., March 14, 1689. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT The preceding Major John Freeman, frequently mentioned 
1 CONT in the public records, first as " Lt.," subsequently as " Capt," 
1 CONT and later as " Major:," and who m. a daughter of Gov. 
1 CONT Prence, was resident in Sandwich, He prob. remained, either 
1 CONT just before or after marriage, only a few years. Dec. 30, 
1 CONT 1649-50, Jonathan Fish of S. deeded to him lands at "Skau- 
1 CONT ton Neck in S., called by the Indians Arquidneck ; " also, 
1 CONT same date, Thomas Dexter conveyed to him " lands at 
1 CONT Ploughed, or Skauton Neck, next to the lands of Edmond 
1 CONT Freeman, Sr., Gent., said lands formerly belonging to Edward 
1 CONT Wallaston, late inhabitant of Sandwich." A few years after, 
1 CONT removed to Eastham, he is mentioned in records as " among 
1 CONT the earliest settlers, with Gov. Prence;" He was somewhat 
1 CONT conspicuous in the military, doing service in the Indian wars. 
1 CONT Through life he was a large landholder. In 1676 the sachem 
1 CONT of Satucket, now West Brewster, conveyed to him, i.e. " John, 
1 CONT Sr., of E.," certain lands; and these were re-conveyed by him, 
1 CONT in 1696, to his son John of H. In 1691 "the town of East- 
1 CONT ham mortgaged to Mr. John Freeman two islands as security 
1 CONT for the payment of 76 pounds, it being the town's proportion of the 
1 CONT expenses of obtaining the new charter from England." In 
1 CONT 1695 another deed from him was made of "lands which 
1 CONT belonged to my father Prence and Bradford, — lands which 
1 CONT belonged to their purchase-grant." Major F. was many years 
1 CONT prominent in public affairs, and has to this day been regarded 
1 CONT as "one of the fathers of Eastham." He was Deputy, from 
1 CONT 1654, eight years; Selectman, from 1663, ten years; Assist- 
1 CONT ant in the Government, from 1666, several years; and late in 
1 CONT life, Dec. 7, 1692, was appointed to the Bench of the Court of 
1 CONT Com. Pleas. Through a long course of years he was a Dea- 
1 CONT con of the Eastham Church. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Major Freeman's wife Mercy d. Sept. 28, 1711, ae. 80. He 
1 CONT d. Oct. 28, 1719, ae. 92. In the graveyard in E. is a head- 
1 CONT stone saying,' " Here lies the body of Maj. John Freeman who 
1 CONT d. Oct. 28, 1719, in the 98th yr. of his age." By the side of 
1 CONT this is another, saying, " Here lies buried the body of Mercy 
1 CONT Freeman, wife to Maj. John Freeman, who d. Sept. 28, 1711, 
1 CONT re. 80." The age on the first tablet is, we are confident, a 
1 CONT mistake, — perhaps the error of the stone-cutter, but in any 
1 CONT event an error in computation. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT Owing to the early demise of several of the numerous 
1 CONT family of Major John Freeman, they are not all individually 
1 CONT mentioned in his will ; which circumstance, together with the 
1 CONT imperfect condition of the public records, has led to mistakes 
1 CONT on the part of some who have aspired to the solution of gene- 
1 CONT alogical difficulties ; and their fancies have led to complications, 
1 CONT making confusion worse confounded. A diligent student will, 
1 CONT however, find all things explicable. The will of Major F. 
1 CONT bears date June 1, 1716, and was pr. Nov. 4, 1719. It men- 
1 CONT tions the " eldest son, then living, " executor ; " and also 
1 CONT "son Edmd. ; Nathaniel, youngest son;" g.-s. Wm. ; sons 
1 CONT John Mayo and Jno. Paine ; drs. Mary Knowles, Hannah 
1 CONT Mayo, Patie Paine, Rebecca Freeman wid. of Thomas, and 
1 CONT g.-dr. Lydia Godfrey. The will provides " freedom for my 
1 CONT Negroes," with " four acres of land, a horse, and a cow ; " and 
1 CONT showing commendable regard for the comfort of the emanci- 
1 CONT pated, says, " I desire my children to put them in such way 
1 CONT that they may not want." An "agreement of heirs" is on 
1 CONT record, signed Jan. 29, 1719-20, by "John, Nathl., Samuel, and 
1 CONT Mary Knowles, Jno. and Hannah Mayo, Patie. Paine, John 
1 CONT Paine, Sarah Freeman widow of Edmd., Isaac eldest son of 
