0 @N1550@ NOTE Pertinent text, v 30(1954), p 128 (editorial note only, undoubtedly by Donald L. Jacobus):
1 CONT "Statements in the past to the effect that the wife of Angel2 Husted married [was?] Rebecca2 Sherwood may well have been based on the terms of the will of Jabez3 Sherwood and the line of reasoning followed by Dr. McCracken above. The present write
1 CONC r has never denied the possibility of this identification of Rebecca and in the previous article cited ante stated that it 'may be correct.' Perhaps it should be pointed out, however, that Jabez Sherwood did not remember all of his brothers and sis
1 CONC ters, nor did he give to any of them his most valuable property with the exception of his Rye estate to his brothcr Stephen. As to his real estate in Greenwich he passed over his immediate relatives in favor of the Husteds. Whether they were his co
1 CONC usins, or simply friends, it may be suggested that, being unmarried, he may have made his home with them. His will failed to specify any relationship to the Husteds or to Jonathan Jessup, whom he also remembered. Jonathan Husted in his own will i
1 CONC n 1705 called Jonathan Jessup his cousin and made him his chief heir, also mentioning that Jonathan Jessup was under 21 and living with him.
1 CONT "These factors strongly indicate that Jabez Sherwood was also a member of the childless Husted household. He may originally have entered that household because of relationship, or there may have been some kind of family connection without actual re
1 CONC lationship. The facts are suggestive but, in this writer's view, are far from proving that Jabez Sherwood was a cousin of Jonathan Husted or that the latter's mother Rebecca was Rebecca Sherwood. It is a very plausible theory that she was, but actu
1 CONC al record proof, or at least further circumstantial evidence pointing in that direction, would be required to remove the identification from the stage of conjecture to that of fact."
0 @N1551@ NOTE Pertinent text, v 73(1998), p 201 (introductory paragraphs only):
1 CONT "The Unidentified Wives of Angell2 Husted and Jonathan2 Reynolds of Greenwich, Connecticut
1 CONT "By Gordon L. Remington, FUGA
1 CONT "In an article entitled 'Rebecca, Wife of Angell Husted, of Greenwich,' George E. McCracken argues that she was Rebecca2 Sherwood, daughter of Thomas1 and Alice Sherwood of Fairfield, Connecticut. McCracken based his argument on an interpretation o
1 CONC f the will of Jabez3 Sherwood (Stephen2, Thomas1), who left all of his land to Jonathan3 Husted (Angell2, Robert1), but did not specify any relationship. McCracken argued that they were first cousins, but Donald Lines Jacobus, in an appended editor
1 CONC ial note, countered that, while the theory was plausible, it was far from conclusive.
1 CONT "This article will demonstrate not only the unlikelihood that Rebecca, wife of Angell2 Husted, was Rebecca Sherwood, but also that Angell's widow Rebecca had been the wife of Jonathan2 Reynolds and that she could not have been the mother of Angell'
1 CONC s children. These conclusions were developed during the course of research on a series of articles on Ange112 Husted's brother entitled 'Robert2 Huestis of Westchester County, New York: His Ancestry and Descendants' currently running in The New Yor
1 CONC k Genealogical and Biographical Record.
1 CONT "The only evidence for the name of Angell2 Husted's wife as Rebecca comes from the inventory of his estate. On 19 April 1706, 'Rebecka Husted widow made oth to the truth of the above Inventory befor Sam" Peck Justic of the peace in Greenwich.' Th
1 CONC e will of Angell Husted, probated on 26 August 1706, mentioned a wife, but did not state her name. It is through the correct interpretation of the language of this will that the conclusions concerning Rebecca were reached."
1 CONT Sources cited, p 201: "George E. McCracken, 'Rebecca, Wife of Angel2 Husted, of Greenwich,' TAG 30(1954):127-28"; :Gordon L. Remington, 'Robert2 Huestis of Westchester County: His Ancestry and Descendants,' NYGBR 129(1998):1-12, 97-108, 191-206 [t
1 CONC o be continued]"; "Inventory and will: Fairfield, Conn., Probate Records, 5:85a."
