0 @N1671@ NOTE p. 4-5  -  JOH N SAND S I 
1 CONT First child of James I and Sarah (Walker) Sands. 
1 CONT BORN: In America, 1649. 
1 CONT MARRIED: Sybil Ray, daughter of Simon III and Mary 
1 CONT (Thomas) Ray, of Block Island. She was born March 
1 CONT 19, 1665; died on Cowneck, December 23, 1733, intestate; interred alongside her husband. 
1 CONT DIED: On the Home Farm, Cowneck, L. I., March 15, 
1 CONT 1712, intestate; interred on Cowneck, in the Sands 
1 CONT graveyard. 
1 CONT ISSUE: John Sands II, of whom later. 
1 CONT SAND S 5 
1 CONT JOHN SANDS I. On May 27, 1674 ,he had a grant of land 
1 CONT in Portsmouth, R. I.; in 1678-80-81, he was deputy to the 
1 CONT General Assembly from Block Island. He removed from 
1 CONT Block Island to Cowneck, afterwards called Sands Point, 
1 CONT bordering on the Sound, about 1691, where he and his brother 
1 CONT purchased adjoining farms. John Sands purchased his 
1 CONT farm from Richard Cornell, of Rockaway, deed dated December 25, 1691, patent taken out by Richard Cornell, in 1686; 
1 CONT the consideration for this and a part of his brother Samuel's 
1 CONT farm was £200. He and his wife both died on the farm he 
1 CONT had purchased, and before his death he gave the family of 
1 CONT Sands a burying ground of about half an acre north of his 
1 CONT mansion; his remains and those of his wife were interred 
1 CONT therein. He left the Home Farm to his son, Nathaniel, his 
1 CONT widow remaining on it until her death. His gravestone, 
1 CONT which is upright and in good condition, has on it the following inscription: 
1 CONT "HERE LIETH YE BODY 
1 CONT OF JOHN SANDS 
1 CONT DIED MARCH YE I 5TH, 
1 CONT 1712 IN YE 63D 
1 CONT YEAR OF HIS AGE." 
1 CONT His wife's gravestone of slate, and upright, still in good condition, bears the following inscription: 
1 CONT "HERE LIETH YE BODY 
1 CONT OF SIBELL YE WIFE 
1 CONT OF JOHN SANDS 
1 CONT DIED DECEM. YE 2 3 
1 CONT 1733 IN YE 68: 
1 CONT YEAR OF HER AGE."
0 @N1672@ NOTE p. 1 - John Sends lst, son of Capt. James of Block Island who came to Mass. about-l637, before his death in 1712 gave to the family of Sandes 1/2 acre for a burying place. His 10 year old son George was buried here in 1704, and John and hi
1 CONC s wife Sybil (Bay) Sands are buried here, their stones upright and plainly readable. Before 1765 John Sands 2nd died, about 18 members of his family had been buried here, many of these oldest stones are still standing.
1 CONT p. 3 - John, Samuel and James Sands came to Cow Neck in 1691 and bought their farms of 500 acres from Richard Cornell. John settled at the bottom of Cow Neck (Sands Point). It is said that the  beautiful little house called the Capt. John Sands Hou
1 CONC se
1 CONT on o1d Sands Point Road was his home.
