0 @N1909@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT VIEW BIOGRAPHICAL ITEMS IN STORIES (MEMORIES) FOR THIS RECORD,9CWB-P8L
1 CONT BIOS: 
1 CONT      (1) See Discussion Item: BIRTH OF THIS JOHN MEAD LIKELY WAS IN ABOUT 1628 IN WATFORD, HERTFORD, ENGLAND;
1 CONT       (2) See biographical data for this John Mead and his parents in excerpts from Article attached as a Story (Memories) to this record.  
1 CONT      (3)  BIOGRAPHICAL SKETCH: John Mead was born in about 1634 in England.  Died on 4 Feb 1699.  Lived in Greenwich, Connecticut.  In 1656, at the age of 22, he married Hannah Potter, daughter of William, a widower of Stamford.  Issue: John, Josep
1 CONC h, Hannah, Ebenezer, Jonathan, David, Benjamin, Nathaniel, Samuel, Abigail, and Mary.   [Source: "Mead-Clark Genealogy," by Eva Mead Firestone, Page 5 of Mead section of this book]
1 CONT 
1 CONT The History and Genealogy of the Mead Family by S.P. Mead says John was born in Fairfield County, CT around 1632-34 [this is before European settlement in Connecticut.  My reading of this source is that it states he was born about 1634, no place li
1 CONC sted.  K. Offill]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Another variation: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Notes for JOHN MEAD: Much of the information in this file is from Mead, Spencer P., LL.B., History and Genealogy
1 CONT of the Mead Family, (New York: The Knickerbocker Press, 1901), but I have attempted to verify information with
1 CONT vital records where they are available.
1 CONT John Mead is known as the founder of the Greenwich, CT Mead family. Old Greenwich was originally settled in
1 CONT 1640 after the land was purchased from Indians by agents of the New Haven Colony. In 1656, Greenwich became
1 CONT part of Stamford. In 1658, John went with his brother Joseph to Hempstead, LI (source: Lee Meade), but returned
1 CONT to Greenwich, CT in 1660. Lee Meade believes he had two children while in Hempstead. John moved from Long
1 CONT Island to Greenwich (Horse-Neck) in 1660, and purchased land from Richard Crab (Deed is dated 26 Oct 1660). He
1 CONT was made a freeman in 1670.
1 CONT From Lee Meade's Newsletter (Vol. 1, No. 4, December 1997) "John had visited land just west of Stamford along
1 CONT the Connecticut coast on the Long Island Sound. It was called Hogsneck, but it was to become the City of
1 CONT Greenwich. On Oct. 26, 1660, John purchased "all the lands and housing" in Greenwich previously belonging to
1 CONT Richard Crabb. In turn, he and six others, known as "The Seven Proprietors," made a request of the General
1 CONT Assembly on Feb. 5, 1664, to separate Greenwich from Stamford, to permit Greenwich to support its own minister
1 CONT and lay out its own lands. It is believed to have been the first recorded Town Meeting for Greenwich. The seven
1 CONT were John Mead, Jonathan Renalds, John Hobby, Joseph Ferris, Joshua Knapp, Angell Husted and Jeffrey Ferris.
1 CONT One of the early rules for the community was that 'none shall be admitted as an inhabitant of Greenwich without a
1 CONT certificate testifying to an orderly life, and he must be approved by the minister.' It was enacted on Dec. 1, 1665."
1 CONT A land grant was given to John on December 30, 1670, described as part of the lowermost meadows lying south of
1 CONT the Westchester Path. He bought more land December 21, 1661in Greenwich, CT from Thomas Studwell. John
1 CONT was a Representative in the Connecticut General Assembly, and is known as one of the original Seven Proprietors
1 CONT who, on 5 Feb 1664, "made a formal request to the General Assembly in Hartford to be allowed to separate from
1 CONT Stamford and to support its own minister and lay out its own lands." The General Assembly declared Greenwich a
1 CONT separate township on 11 May 1665 and authorized funds to hire an orthodox minister. In 1672, the so-called "27
1 CONT Proprietors" (including John Mead) bought land from the Indians and it was laid out for home lots and granted to the
1 CONT 27 men (Mead, 1911). "John Mead was a prosperous and self-possessed man with a strong character" (Collins,
1 CONT 1999, p. 58).
1 CONT (Vance Mead has his dates as 1628-1699) He had two wills. In the first will, dated March 16, 1694/95, he
1 CONT mentioned only his sons and bequeathed land to each one; in the second he bequeathed money to his children and
1 CONT grandson, John (Fairfield Probate Records, Vol 1, p. 196).
1 CONT Notes for HANNAH POTTER: Hannah was either the daughter or stepdaughter of William Potter of Stamford."
1 CONT 
1 CONT http://collectornuts.com/mead.pdf
