0 @N1956@ NOTE Nicholas Webster arrived in the "second wave" to Stamford in the 1660s-1670s. in the 1680s a group of about 20 men saw opportunity in the very north of Stamford, in what was called  the Hop Grounds. Nicolas Webster was one of these "proprie
1 CONC tors" and purchased the land from Catonah. This settlement was recognized by Hartford in 1682 as Bedford, Conn. Colony.   In 1683 Bedford and land known later as the "Oblong" were turned over to New York by Connecticut in return for clear claim t
1 CONC o Greenwich and Stamford.
1 CONT Nicholas Webster was in Stamford about 1673, moved to Bedford in 1680, but returned to Stamford in 1685. He lived in the eastern part of Stamford.
1 CONT Nicholas Webster married Sarah (Waterbury) Dibble. Sarah Waterbury was the da. of John Waterbury and Rose.
1 CONT Sarah actually divorced her first husband Zachariah Dibble in 1672 on ground of "desertion and infidelity". Sarah's 2nd Husband was Nicholas Webster, they married about 1673. They had 3 known living children; 1.John -bc 1674,d 1716, m 1702 Sarah Ja
1 CONC gger (bc 1677, d 1760, d/o Jeremiah), she m2 James Slason;      2. David-bc-1676,d 1732,m1 1706 Abigail Holly (d 1707, prob d/o John), m2 1709 Mercy Morehouse (d/o Jonathan).  3. Rachel- m1 1708 Henry Atwood, m2 1732 or 1733 Nathan Sturges.
