0 @N2156@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Edward Bunn of Massachusetts was first bearer of the Bunn name in America. The first American record of him is found at Nantasket (now Hull) in 1642 when his right as a proprietor was acknowledged. On October 30, 1662, he deposed as follows: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Edward Bunn, aged 50 years or thereabouts, and Thomas Collier, aged 40 years or thereabouts, do testify that ever since we came to Nantasket to dwell we have possessed Brewster Islands by virtue of the grant of the General court, without any lega
1 CONC l molestation of any kind, and in this manner", etc.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT The "History of the Bunn Family of America" (James Ellis, ed., 1928) erred in its assumption that Edward Bunn had a first marriage and children by such marriage.  The only marriage record for him is found in the records of Boston, reporting that: E
1 CONC dward Bunn of Hull, widower, married to Elizabeth Mason June 20, 1657.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT Contrary to the notion that he had any children, Edward left a will in which he left his entire estate to the widow and to three "cousins."  If he had had surviving children, they would have at least been mentioned in the will.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT The record of Elizabeth Bunn who married at Boston, in October 1673, Joseph Howe of that city.  Edward Bunn's will was dated 14 April 1673,and it was proved on 20 October ("eighth month") of that year - the same month in which his widow married Jos
1 CONC eph Howe.   
1 CONT 
1 CONT Specifically, in Edward's will, he made bequests to his cousin James Mason, then living with him; his cousin Samuel Lee; his wife Elizabeth; and his cousin Sarah Mason.  Possibly by "cousin" he meant "step son" and "step daughter," as the term "cou
1 CONC sin" was used for a wide variety of relationships in that time.
