0 @N2234@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Jonathan Reynolds actual probate record and photocopy of his will from 1673 can be found in the Fairfield Connecticut Probate Records. It states that his wife was Rebecca, daughter of Angell Husted. In the book John and Sarah Reynolds of Watertown
1 CONC , it mentions that John and Sarah's son Jonathan Reynold's father-in-law was Angell Husted, this backs up the will, which is all we really have to go on. It also states Angell's wife was Rebecca and they had a daughter named Rebecca.
1 CONT 
1 CONT But here is an example of poor research, in the book John and Sarah Reynolds of Watertown, it states that their daughter Elizabeth, married a man named Peter Ferris, who help condemn a woman named Mercy Disborough to death for withcraft. *You can f
1 CONC ind the story of Mercy Disborough on the historical site for Stamford CT. The truth is, she was on trial for withcraft, and vowed for by other town folk to be a woman of peace and not convicted.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT *As, far as Gordon Remington, please do not rely on his research, it was done a long time ago. What he has stated and what's written in the Great Migration can only be proven if they provide a written document from that time period or a copy of a w
1 CONC ritten document from that time period that is on file in a courthouse. Without that, it's just a story.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Gordon Remington made a huge mistake with other colonial ancestors of mine, and it was a painstaking process to correct it, but I was able to, because I found actual document's from the 1600's and 1700's. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT CAUTION: INSTEAD OF PERPETUATING OLD MISTAKES & MIXING HIM WITH OTHER DIFFERENT PEOPLE, PLEASE READ THE LATEST SCHOLARSHIP in Sources. Angell Husted of Greenwich, Conn, made his will April 5, 1706 which was proven April 19, 1706, in which he name
1 CONC s his wife Rebecca and states that his sons Jonathan, Joseph and Angell have had their share of his estate; names other children, Elizabeth Baldwin, Moses, John, Samuel and makes his son Samuel and his son-in-law Jonathan Reynolds his administrator
1 CONC s. (Fairfield Probate Records. Copy in Long Island Historical Society, pg.160)
1 CONT 
1 CONT From The Great Migration Begins:   ANGELL HUESTIS, b. say 1624; m. (1) by about 1645 _____ _____; m. (2) after 1673 Rebecca (_____) Reynolds, widow of Jonathan Reynolds 
1 CONT 
1 CONT [TAG 73:201-6].(1) VIEW BIOGRAPHICAL ITEMS IN STORY (MEMORIES) FOR THIS RECORD, LR63-NTW.
1 CONT (2) Problem of identifying his unknown first wife, she is NOT Rebecca Sherwood, and his second wife and widow, Rebecca, who is possibly the widow of Jonathan Reynolds - discussed in Article: "Rebecca Revisited: The Unidentified Wives of Angell Hust
1 CONC ed and Jonathan Reynolds of Greenwich, Connecticut,' by Gordon L. Remington
1 CONT (3) , New England Marriages prior to 1700 lists Angell Husted (?1620-1706) & Rebecca (?Sherwood) before 1656, before 1645 in Greenwich, CT. The ? means Sherwood could be incorrect, but it is there because it is a possibility.  It is permissible fo
1 CONC r Angell Husted to be linked to two different wives.  [BUT SEE MORE RECENT SCHOLARLY RESEARCH BY GORDON REMINGTON]
0 @N2235@ NOTE History of Greenwich, early settlers, genealogy records on multiple families including Husted Family
0 @N2236@ NOTE Angell married after 1673, as his 2nd, and as her 2nd, to Rebecca (Unknown) Reynolds, widow of Jonathan Reynolds.[3]
1 CONT Remington also shows that Rebecca (Unknown) Reynolds (widow of Jonathan Reynolds) was Angel's second wife and not the mother of Angell's children.[3] Mahler[4] builds on his work, and it was referenced by Anderson in The Great Migration.[2]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children of Angell and his unidentified 1st wife (note the birth dates were "arbitrarily assigned by Mead"):[3][5]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jonathan Husted, b. ca. 1647; m. Mary Lockwood.
1 CONT David Husted, b. ca. 1649; d. sp. prior to 1706.
1 CONT Joseph Husted, b. ca. 1652; m. Sarah ___
1 CONT Angell Husted, Jr., b. ca. 1656; m. twice.
1 CONT Elizabeth Husted, b. ca. 1656; m. ___ Baldwin.
1 CONT Moses Husted, b. ca. 1659; m. Susanna ___
1 CONT John Husted, b. ca. 1662; m. Mary ___
1 CONT Samuel Husted, b. 1665; m. 1st Elizabeth ___, 2nd Sarah Knapp.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Second Wife Rebecca
1 CONT 
1 CONT Angell Husted mentioned his wife in his will, but did not state her name.[3] Our only evidence of her name is from the inventory of Angell Husted's estate:[3]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Rebecca Husted widow made oath to the truth of the above Inventory before Samll Peck Justice of the peace in Greenwich.[3] [6]
1 CONT Rebecca was once assumed to have been a daughter of Angell Husted. Mead in Ye Historie of ye Town of Greenwich ... reports an eldest daughter named Rebecca,[7] and he was followed by Elizabeth Leach Richford in Three Hundred Colonial Ancestors ...[
1 CONC 8] This was based on the misunderstanding of Angell's will in which he mentioned his "son-in-law," Jonathan Reynolds. Jonathan's wife's name was Rebecca, so "Rebecca Husted" was invented to explain why Jonathan Reynolds was called "son-in-law." Rem
1 CONC ington points out that the term "son-in-law" was used as we use step-son today. In fact, Jonathan Reynold's widowed mother, Rebecca (Unknown) Reynolds, was the second wife of Angel Husted, making Jonathan Reynolds the step-son of Angell Husted.[3
1 CONC ] Angell Husted had no daughter named Rebecca.
1 CONT 
1 CONT She has also been assumed to have been Rebecca Sherwood (daughter of Thomas and Alice (Tiler) Sherwood), but Gordon Remington convincingly argues this is chronologically impossible.[3
