0 @N2261@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT 
1 CONT (edited) 
1 CONT 
1 CONT THE PARENTS OF RICHARD ELLIS ARE UNKNOWN. We only know he was son of a widow Ellis who married second to Lambert Genery (aka Chenery). He had a brother Joseph Ellis and two half-brothers John and Isaac Chenery. Please do not attach any other parent
1 CONC s. Richard Ellis, in Holman, Mary Lovering, and Harriett Grace Scott. "The Scott Genealogy" (Boston, Mass., 1919), page 193: "Richard Ellis, Lieutenant, born about 1621, deposed on 28 January 1661, aged 40 years, on 28 May 1685, aged 64 years, die
1 CONC d in Dedham, Masschussets, 21 October 1694; … Richard Ellis presumably came to this country with his mother and step-father Lambert Genery and was a resident of Dedham by 1636. He was a wheelwright by trade, but was a man of good education, as wa
1 CONC s also his younger brother Joseph, who served Dedham as its schoolmaster for a number of years. He was made sergeant of the military company of Dedham in 1659, holding this office until after 1683, when he became a lieutenant. In 1659 he was a wood
1 CONC reeve, in 1660 served on a committee with Lt. Fisher to treat with the Indians, and later was on many other important committees. On 8 June 1692, he was made a deputy to the Massachusetts General Court. In 1671, Richard Ellis, in behalf of himsel
1 CONC f and Isaac Genery, notified the town of their hope of having found a copper mine.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Administration on his estate was granted to his widow Elizabeth and to John Ellis, his eldest son, 27 December. 1694, and the account of John Ellis, surviving administrator of his father Richard Ellis was filed 16 September 1697 (Suffolk Probate, 3
1 CONC :516; 11:339.)
1 CONT 
1 CONT https://archive.org/stream/scottgenealogyde00holm#page/192/mode/2up
1 CONT 
1 CONT Richard Ellis was involved with his half -rother Isaac Genery in the settlement of Wrentham.
1 CONT   
1 CONT "John Ellice coming together wth Lambert Genery, the said Lambert Declareing that thier coming was to demaund sattisfactio for one pcell of Meadowe, formerly granted to Jo. Ellice the Towne. by ye select men. pmise & tender to sattisfie the grante
1 CONC . made to the said John & in conueanient season to giue him posession therof."
1 CONT 
1 CONT Source: "The Early Records of the Town of Dedham, Massachusetts 1636 - 1659," p. 165. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 6 May (the 6th of ye 3 month called Maye) 1642: Dedham Early Records of the town: Granted to Sam Morse, Daniel Morse and Rich Ellis- "ech of them to haue a pcell of vpland In ye greate Iland wthin ye lyne ther what may be conveniently ther had at y
1 CONC e discretion of those men deputed to lay out ye same." 
1 CONT 
1 CONT "A Genealogical Dictionary of The First Settlers of New England, Before 1692, Volume #2," Elkins - England   by James Savage
1 CONT 
1 CONT "ELLIS  ... RICHARD, Dedham, by w. Elizabeth m. 1650, wh. may seem to be d. of Lambert Genery, espec. as G. in his will calls E. s.-in-law, had, perhaps sev. ch. but certain. a d. b. 1651; and Mary, 3 Feb. 1655, wh. m. 12 Mar. 1680, Amos Fisher. "
1 CONT 
1 CONT --"Looking for any paternal information for a Richard Ellis who was born in Suffolk, England, around 1621. His father's name listed is George Ellis but it appears George who married Mary Lovering Holman died a young death. Mary remarried a Lamber
1 CONC t Genery and took her young Ellis children to America. George Ellis would have been born around 1590 and died around 1630 to 1635."--
1 CONT 
1 CONT Good researchers have been trying for decades to determine Richard's father, to no avail. Can someone please identify a source for the above mentioned George and Mary?  The Mary Lovering Holman seems to make reference to the author of the "Scott Ge
1 CONC nealogy," published 1919!  A MyHeritage Family Tree seems to have this combination of George and Mary, but I do not have access to MyHeritage so I can not determine where this information came from, if there are any primary sources to back it up, o
1 CONC r if it's just a guess.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The parents were found in My Heritage and Family Search. Records must have been added since the above was written.
