1 CONC y Townsend are unknown.  BEWARE OF OLD ERRORS CIRCULATING ONLINE.  In order to be a witness for the christening of his nephew in 1642 at the Dutch Reformed Church at New Amsterdam, Henry had to be born by about 1620.  He was also older than his bro
1 CONC ther Richard, who was born say 1624. The Oyster Bay Townsend family has been proven by DNA testing to have no relationship to the family of Lynn, Essex, Massachusetts nor is there proof the family came from Norfolk although that tradition was hel
1 CONC d by the family when they wrongly assumed they were sons of Thomas of Lynn, Massachusetts.  That theory is now disproved by DNA results for both families.  It is an error to assume he was a son of any Thomas of anywhere.  Henry was first of recor
1 CONC d in New Amsterdam when he witnessed his nephew's baptism in 1642.  He was married by 1653 or possibly sooner.  Henry I married Ann Coles, daughter of Robert.  Their children were:
