0 @N2455@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT CAUTION PLEASE READ ALL to avoid perpetuating an old persistent error.  Richard Smith's wife's name was Sarah Hammond so please leave her here with this name.  See full corrective argument in NYGBR 121:19-22.  See also Robert Charles Anderson, "Gre
1 CONC at Migration Begins," v. 2, G-O, page 852.  This source gives her Marie Hammont's christening record so perhaps she was christened Marie.  
1 CONT Great Migration Source link:
1 CONT http://interactive.ancestry.com/2496/42521_b158319-00175?pid=57708&backurl=//search.ancestry.com//cgi-bin/sse.dll?_phsrc%3DeOc4%26_phstart%3DsuccessSource%26usePUBJs%3Dtrue%26indiv%3D1%26db%3DGreatMigration%26gss%3Dangs-d%26new%3D1%26rank%3D1%26msT
1 CONC %3D1%26gsfn%3DWilliam%26gsfn_x%3DNN%26gsln%3DHammond%26gsln_x%3DNN%26MSAV%3D1%26uidh%3D5v2%26pcat%3D40%26fh%3D0%26h%3D57708%26recoff%3D4%25205%26ml_rpos%3D1&treeid=&personid=&hintid=&usePUB=true&_phsrc=eOc4&_phstart=successSource&usePUBJs=true#?ima
1 CONC geId=42521_b158319-00177
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sarah Hammond was christened on 12 December 1621 at the Church of Saint Peter and Saint Paul in Lavenham, Suffolk, England. Her baptism has been confused with that of her sister Mary Hammond, also a daughter of William Hammond and Elizabeth Paine
1 CONC , who was baptized on 21 October 1623 ("Marie daughter of William Hammont").
1 CONT 
1 CONT As a girl, Sarah Hammond came to America with her family on the Francis, sailing from Ipswich on 21 April 1634, and arriving in Boston in June 1634.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Although her marriage began inauspiciously when her intended, Richard Smith, sued to be freed from his proposal and lost, the union appears to have prospered.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Mother of nine children.
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Balance wheel for the impetuosity of an aggressive and ... visionary husband" (The Family of Richard Smith of Smithtown, Long Island, by Richard Kinsman Smith, 1967, page 26).
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 1 July 1662, "my daughter Sarah Smith" and her son Adam Smith were mentioned in the will of William Hammond of Watertown, Massachusetts.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Mrs. Sarah Smith died after 20 January 1708, the date of the writing of her will.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1 CONT 
1 CONT Richard Smith and Sarah Hammond were the parents of the following children.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 1. Jonathan Smith, born about 1641.
1 CONT 2. Elizabeth Smith, born about 1643.
1 CONT 3. Richard Smith, born about 1645.
1 CONT 4. Job Smith, born about 1647.
1 CONT 5. Adam Smith, born in 1649.
1 CONT 6. Obadiah Smith, born in 1651.
1 CONT 7. Samuel Smith, born in 1654.
1 CONT 8. Daniel Smith, born about 1656.
1 CONT 9. Deborah Smith, born about 1658.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
1 CONT 
1 CONT The identification of Sarah wife of Richard Smith of Smithtown as Sarah Hammond daughter of William Hammond and Elizabeth Paine of Watertown was first published in "The Identity of Sarah, Wife of Richard₁ Smith of Smithtown, Long Island" by Edwar
1 CONC d H. L. Smith III in The New York Genealogical and Biographical Record, volume 121, pages 19 – 22 (1990).
1 CONT 
1 CONT The Great Migration Begins, volume 2, pages 850 – 854 (1995) further discusses the family of William Hammond and Elizabeth Paine, but it supplants the baptismal date for Sarah Hammond (12 December 1621) with that of her sister Mary Hammond (21 Oc
1 CONC tober 1623) without explanation.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Some earlier works referred to her as "Sarah Folger" without substantiation.
