0 @N2593@ NOTE Records of divorce of Peter Tallman and Ann Hill.
1 CONT Partial Transcription:
1 CONT P. 85, October 1664.  Upon the consideration of Peter Tallmans petition, wherein hee complaines that by reason of the insolent carriadge [sic] of Thomas Durfee with the sayd Tallmans wife, and the danger therein he expresseth hee stands in by reaso
1 CONC n thereof, &c.: the Court doe order that the sayd Durfee be sent for, and admonished for his unsuitable carriadge before the Court. 
1 CONT       pp. 122-124, May 3, 1665.  … Whereas, Peter Tollman presented a petition to this Assembly, wherein hee desires this Court to grant him a divorse upon grownds in his said petition alleaged; upon which the Court called for Ann Tollman befor
1 CONC e them, and the aforesaid petition being read before her, and the question being asked what she did say to that which was said concearning her in the said petition: … she did in open Court confesse, that it was the truth that the child was none o
1 CONC f his begetting, and that it was befotten by another man.
1 CONT      The Court having heard the confession of the abovesaid Ann Tollman, and considering the premises:
1 CONT       It is ordered by free vote of the Assembly, that she … is an adulteress.
1 CONT      The Court having well and considerately wayed [weighed] what has been declared by way of petition by Peter Tollman against Ann, his wife; and her confession upon her examination that she is guilty of adultery, &c.  Upon all which, the Court do
1 CONC e adjudge the said Ann Tollman to receive the penalty that the law hath provided for such an offence;  that is, whipping and fine, according to the law made May 22, 1655 [sic]; which law doth determine that the Island, shall be whipt a Portsmouth
1 CONC , receiving fifteene stripes; and after a weeke respite, t be whipt at Newport receiving fifteene stripes, and to pay a fine of ten pound to the Gennerall Treasurer: and the said Peter Tollman is adjudged to have his bill of divorce granted him. 
1 CONT      Ordered, that Ann Tollman, expressed in the aforesaid order, is appointed to receive the first punishment at Portsmouth, on Munday, being the 22d day of this instant May; and the next Munday following, being the 29th day instant, to receive th
1 CONC e second punishment of fifteene stripes at Newport;  and in the meane time to remaine in prison is safe cutodye, as alsoe till the fine be payd. 
1 CONT      Upon a petition of Ann Tollman, wherein she desires mercy from the Court, &c., and she being called before the Court, and questioned whether she did intend in her petition to return to her husband, to which her answer was, that she would rathe
1 CONC r cast herself on the mercy of God if he take away her live, than to returne.  Upon which the sentence of the Court is, that they see noe cause to reverse the sentence formerly gone forth against her.
1 CONT      Pp. 187-8, 1 May 1667 [two years later].  Whereas, Ann, late wife to Peter Talman, of Portsmouth, was at the Generall Assembly, held May 3, 1665, sentenced to be whipt at the towne of Portsmouth, and at the towne of Newport, and she at the tim
1 CONC e escapeinge the execution of that sentence:  and this Assembly being informed that she is now againe come into the Collony, it is ordered, that warrant shall be forthwith issued forth from this Assembly to any constable forthwith, to apprehend th
1 CONC e body of sayd Ann, and bring her before this Assembly, in which warrant the Constable shall be authorized, if need be, to command and require suffitient [sic] ayde;  … 
1 CONT      Whereas, Anthony Emery, Cosntable for the towne of Portsmouth, who by warrant from this Court, was commanded to apprehend and bringe the body of Ann, …; 
1 CONT      Whereas, the aforesaid Ann Talman was at the Gererall Assembly, held the 3d of May, 1665, sentenced to be twice whipt, which by her escape was not then executed;  and whereas she hath petitoned this Assambly for mercy, the Court doe adree an
1 CONC d order, that one halfe of her punishment shall be remitted, soe that she shall be but once whipt with fifteen stripes at the towne of Newport; … and the fine she was then fyned is wholly remitted.
