0 @N2609@ NOTE Edward Smith cites Henry Hoff, editor of NYGBR, published in "The American Genealogist," 64[1989]:47-51, [TAG] recent findings that show the wife of Richard Smith of Smithtown to be Sarah Hammond of Watertown, dau of William Hammond. Smit
1 CONC h further describes the primary source records that prove the relationship. In William Hammond's will dated 1 July 1662 (at Watertown, MA), mentions 5 daughters including Sarah Smith and also names her son Adam Smith. William Hammond also mentione
1 CONC d his daughter Hannah (or Anne) Barnes, widow of Timothy(1) Hawkins.  William Hammond's wife was Elizabeth Paine. There is a 1640 letter from William Paine to John Winthrop regarding a court case in which Richard Smith was apparently trying to brea
1 CONC k his commitment to the marriage. This is corroborated by another letter to Winthrop from Rev. George Phillips, pastor at Watertown and Winthrop's notation that Smith lost the case and he and Sarah Hammond were married. In 1700, Sarah's son Daniel
1 CONC , as a widower went to Watertown and md. a widow "Ruhannah (Johnson) Hawkins of Watertown, MA." Ruhannah's first husband Timothy Hawkins would have been a first cousin to Daniel Smith, giving a logical reason for this marriage to have occurred. Th
1 CONC e fact that Timothy's mother was Hannah or Anne, a sister to Sarah (Hammond) Smith of Smithtown, and that all these relationships are confirmed by their father William Hammond's will and by other evidence just cited, seems with strong foundation. I
1 CONC n other sources, there was no proof whatsoever that Richard Smith ever md. a Sarah Folger or that such a person ever existed. John Folger was the first of the name on Long Island and it is known he had a daughter Mary who md. Peter Paine of Southol
1 CONC d, but there is no record of a daughter Sarah. And the NYGBR article here cited does offer clear and compelling evidence.
