1 CONT I. John Waterbury, of Watertown, Mass., was born Circa i6i5. He sold house and eight acres of land at Watertown, October 1 5th, 1646, to Robert Pierce, of Watertown. He removed to Stamford, Conn., about the year 1646, and was one of the first settl
1 CONC ers of the place. He died there 31st of July, i65S. His wife was named Rose; their five children were as follows —
1 CONT 
1 CONT 2. Rachael Parcett Waterbury, born at Watertown, Mass., Circa 1640. 
1 CONT 2. Sarah Waterbury, born at Watertown, Mass.,  Circa 1646. 
1 CONT 2. John Waterbury, born at Stamford, Conn., Circa 1650.
1 CONT 2. Jonathan Waterbury, born at Stamford, Conn., Circa 1653. 
1 CONT 2. David Waterbury, born at Stamford, Conn., Circa 1635. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT (Rose, his widow, was married secondly, 11nth of May, 1659, to Joseph Garnsey, and had a son Joseph, born 30th of June, 1662).
1 CONT John Waterbury prized the property left by Vincent Simpkins. in November, 1653.
1 CONT 
1 CONT At a General Court, held in New Haven, in June, 1657, a petition was presented from Greenwich to become a part of Stamford, by Richard Law, John Waterbury, and George Slawson, Deputies from Stamford. — [History of Stamford, page Sr.
1 CONT Agreement made in 1652, between John Bagot, Duty, and John Waterbury, concerning lands.
1 CONT 
1 CONT John Waterbury had lands in Stamford, Conn., as early as 1650, as appears from assignment of lands of that date.  His inventory bears date in April, 1659, amounting to  185 British pounds  I2S.
1 CONT His sons were John, Jonathan and David.
1 CONT Those three made over to their stepfather, Joseph Garnsey, in 1674, a parcel of land then in possession of John Miller.
1 CONT His widow had married Joseph Garnsey, in May, 1659. when she attested his will.
1 CONT Waterbury is one of the most numerous as well as respectable of the Stamford names down to the present day. [History of Stamford, page 64.]
