0 @N2718@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Thomas Snow (Jr.) was born in 1600 in Chelmsford, Essex, England. His father is said to have been Thomas Snow Sr., born about 1575 in Stock, Essex, England. His mother's name is not known.[1]
1 CONT In 1636, Thomas Snow married Melatiah (aka Milcah) Kelway, daughter of Walter Kelway. She was 18 years old; he was 35 years old. They married in Chelmsford, Essex, England, just before emigrating together from England to the Massachusetts Bay Colon
1 CONC y. They arrived in Boston MA in 1636 (part of the Winthrop Fleet).
1 CONT They were non-conformists or Puritans. Thomas Snow is known to have been named a Freeman in Boston on May 18, 1642. He was admitted to the Congregational Church (Puritan) either on April 18, 1643 or Sep. 5, 1644.[2]
1 CONT Neither Walter Kelway nor any of his 5 other children are known to have emigrated to New England at that time.
1 CONT Thomas Snow was a barber (surgeon) in Boston, Massachusetts. He and his wife also ran a small tavern/inn, called "The Sign of the Dove" at their home.[3]
1 CONT Thomas Snow wrote his will on November 10, 1668; it was proven on April 28, 1669, shortly after his death (the practice at that time was for Wills to be made shortly before death and proven immediately afterwards, almost always within 10 days). H
1 CONC e named his wife Milcah Snow as administrator so we know she survived him. The following is from Charles Henry Pope's book, "Pioneers of Massachusetts," Boston: 1900:
1 CONT SNOW, Thomas, barber Boston, administrator [of a] charter house [pub], Sept. 5, 1644, from May 18, 1642. Wife Milcah adm. chh. [as of] May 18, 1642. They mortgaged their house, to which the Sign of the Dove was fixed, 16 Dec. 1667. Thomas Snow wa
1 CONC s an American patriot. He signed the 1664 "Boston Petitions" to King Charles II pleading that he not revoke the colony's traditional liberties, won during the Commonwealth period 1649-1662.[5] 
1 CONT Thomas Snow, d. in 1668-9. Will dated 10 Nov. 1688 [sic=1668], prob. April 28, 1669. Wife Milcha to dispose of the estate at her discretion among his children. [Reg. XX, 240.][6] 
1 CONT Thomas Snow, "The Sign of the Dove," Boston, MA, ante-1615 - 1668/69.[7] 
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT     1. Ties that Bind Us, on genes2.com. States Thomas Jr. had a sister: Katheryn Snow(e), chr. Nov 15, 1607 in Stock, Essex, England. Stock & Chelmsford are just 6 miles apart.
1 CONT     2. Families from MD, VA, DC as well as VT, MA, NY - Thomas Snow
1 CONT     3. NCBertie L-Archives - The Flagon and Trencher: Descendants of Colonial Tavern Keepers, Vol. 8 - Thomas Snow
1 CONT     4. Ancestors of DONALD ANDERSON GARDNER - Thomas Snow Note: Milcah & Melatiah were the same daughter although some genealogies list them separately. Others do not include Hannah, who probably died at birth; she was a twin.
1 CONT     5. "Proceedings of the Massachusetts Historical Society," Volume 52, by Massachusetts Historical Society: 1919, pg. 315.
1 CONT     6. "Pioneers of Massachusetts," p. 425.
1 CONT     7. NCBertie L-Archives - The Flagon and Trencher: Descendants of Colonial Tavern Keepers, Vol. 8 - Thomas Snow
