0 @N2736@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Life History of Major John Freeman (1626–1719)
1 CONT Birth and Early Years
1 CONT John Freeman was born on 28 January 1626 in Billingshurst, Sussex, England, and christened in Pulborough. He was the son of Edmund Freeman and Bennett Hodsoll, a family of good standing in Sussex. In 1635, at about eight years old, John emigrated t
1 CONC o New England aboard the ship Abigail with his parents and siblings, arriving in Boston on 8 October 1635. The Freeman family first settled in Saugus (Lynn) and later moved to Sandwich in 1637.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Marriage and Family
1 CONT On 13 February 1649/50, John Freeman married Mercy Prence, daughter of Governor Thomas Prence and Patience Brewster (granddaughter of Elder William Brewster of the Mayflower). This marriage united two prominent families of Plymouth Colony. John an
1 CONC d Mercy had eleven children, including John, Thomas, Patience, Hannah, Edmund, Mercy, William, Prence, Nathaniel, and Bennet Freeman. These children and their descendants became influential in Cape Cod and beyond.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Settlement and Civic Life
1 CONT John Freeman became one of the founders of Eastham, moving there around 1644 when his father-in-law, Governor Prence, relocated from Duxbury. Freeman was deeply involved in Eastham’s civic and religious life:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Deacon of the Eastham Church for many years
1 CONT Selectman for ten years
1 CONT Deputy to the General Court for eight years
1 CONT Assistant to the Governor from 1666 onward
1 CONT Judge of the Court of Common Pleas (appointed 1692)
1 CONT 
1 CONT His estate grew to include extensive land holdings in Eastham, Harwich, Truro, and Narragansett (now Gorham, Maine), the latter granted for his military service.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Military Service
1 CONT Freeman served in the Sandwich militia as early as 1643. By 1654/55, he was made ensign of the Eastham Company, later promoted to Lieutenant (1659), Captain, and eventually Major of the Cape Regiment in 1685. He fought in King Philip’s War (1675
1 CONC –1676), a pivotal conflict between colonists and Native tribes, and was recognized for his leadership.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Character and Legacy
1 CONT Contemporary records describe John Freeman as “a worthy gentleman, very pious, and very able for his office.” He was known for his integrity, leadership, and devotion to his community. His life spanned from the early struggles of Plymouth Colon
1 CONC y to the stability of the Province of Massachusetts Bay.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Death and Burial
1 CONT Major John Freeman died on 28 October 1719 in Eastham at the age of 93. He was buried in Cove Burying Ground, where his gravestone remains a testament to his prominence. His wife Mercy had predeceased him in 1711; her gravestone is noted for its he
1 CONC art-shaped design with an epitaph engraved in small capital letters.
1 CONT 
1 CONT References
1 CONT 
1 CONT Genealogical Notes of Barnstable Families by Otis (1888)
1 CONT Mayflower Planters by Hills (1936)
1 CONT Mayflower Families Through Five Generations, Vol. 24
1 CONT The Great Migration Begins by Robert C. Anderson
1 CONT Plymouth Colony Vital Records
1 CONT Barnstable County Probate Records, Vol. 3
