0 @N2743@ NOTE Pertinent text: 
1 CONT "6. JOSEPH BENJAMIN 2. (John 1), born September 16, 1633 at Newtowne, (now Cambridge) , Mass., the son of John and Abigail (Eddye) Benjamin; died April (spring of) 1704 at Preston, Conn. He m/1 Jemima Lumbert (Lumbard/Lambert, etc.) of Barnstable
1 CONC , Mass on June 10, 1661. She was the dau. of Thomas and Joice (---) Lumbert and was bc. 1636 at Dorchester, Mass. and died after March 7, 1664 and more probably in 1666 with the birth of her second daughter. He m/2 Sarah Clarke of Yarmouth, Mass. p
1 CONC revious to Dec. 7, 1668 and more probably soon after the death of his first wife, since he had 2 very small children to care for.  She was the dau. of Lt. William and Sarah (Lumbert-Lumbard) Clarke. She was born Aug. 1, 1639 and was still living i
1 CONC n 1716. 
1 CONT "William Clarke of Yarmouth died Dec. 7, 1668 and gave his property to Joseph Benjamin by nuncupative will. The estate was valued at 8.3.0 pounds. Sarah Clarke's mother was probably a sister to Thomas and Barnard Lumbert, which would make the two w
1 CONC ives cousins. This, too, would account for Barnard Lumbert, age 60, testifying as to his presence and hearing the will of William Clarke. 
1 CONT "In Savage's account of the Lumbert family, he indicates that the first marriage of Joseph Benjamin may have been 'a run-away marriage'.
1 CONT "Joseph was a watchman at Charlestown in 1665 and was named in the sessions (court rec). He lived in Barnstable and then sometime before 1668 he moved to Yarmouth, He is mentioned in the Annals of Yarmouth in Oct. 3, 1663 and Apr. 29, 1673. In Yarm
1 CONC outh, his farm was near the Meadows. In 1680 he exchanged his farm for that of Joseph Gorham in Barnstable. He sold a farm in Cambridge, which he inherited, on Oct. 30, 1686. 
1 CONT "The town of Preston, Conn, was established in 1686 and Joseph and his family moved there in 1696, where he bought 100 acres of land. His abstract of purchase was made July 27, 1696 and is as follows: 'John Stanton Sr., of Stonington, New London Co
1 CONC . and Colony of Conn., for 13 pounds 12 shillings, sold to Joseph Benjamin, now resident of the Town of Preston, same county and Colony, 100 acres of upland, fresh meadow land and swamp in the township of Preston...' and then follows a descriptio
1 CONC n of the boundaries of the land. This deed was signed by John and Hannah Stanton and witnessed by Benjamin Howard, Elizabeth Mason, and Joseph Freeman, and is recorded in Vol. 1 p. 273 of the Preston Land Records. 
1 CONT "The trees on the boundaries bore the mark JB for Joseph Benjamin and TP for Thomas Parke, and it was on this land that Joseph built his house. The land remained in the possession of the Benjamin descendants until the late 1800's. 
1 CONT "In 1703 the father followed the common custom, and deeded his lands to his two sons, Joseph and John.  On June 24, 1703 Joseph Benjamin of Preston, for various reasons but especially for natural affection to son Joseph Benjamin, deeded to said son
1 CONC , part of the lands southward of Pachug River, with Jonathan Tracy and Isaac Averill being the witnesses. On July 27, 1703 Joseph Benjamin deeded the other part of his land to his son John Benjamin, who was to possess the lands after the father's d
1 CONC eath, 'only allowing my wife a third part of said farm during her widowhood'. Both deeds were acknowledged on July 27, 1703 and recorded May 8, 1704, after Joseph's death. (Land records of Preston, Conn: 1-369, 370). 
1 CONT "Joseph Benjamin died in the spring of 1704 and an inventory of his estate was taken April 27, 1704 by Jonathan Tracy, Samuel Lennerson, and Thomas Stanton. It included only household furniture and clothing. The names and ages of the children are g
1 CONC iven in the inventory: viz: Joseph, age 30, John, age 22; Abigail, Jemima, Sarah, Hannah, Marah, Marcy: all above 20 years. On June 5, 1704 the Widow Sarah Benjamin personally appeared and made oath to the truth of the Inventory (Probate Rec. New L
1 CONC ondon, Conn. Book A p. 26l). At the Court of Probate held at New London on June 9» 1704, the inventory of the estate of Joseph Benjamin of Preston, dec'd, was exhibited, proved, accepted, and ordered to be recorded and the Court granted power of a
1 CONC dministration to the widow and relict of said Benjamin, and defers distribution for the present. (Probate Journal, Vol. 1 p. 30). Only one document is now on file of this estate, and that is the inventory. 
1 CONT "Children of Joseph and Jemima (Lumbard) Benjamin:
1 CONT "28. Abigail Benjamin b. March 23, 1662 at Charlestown, Mass. (vr). She d. unmarried after 1704. 
1 CONT "*29. Jemima Benjamin b. Feb. 1666 prob. at Barnstable. 
1 CONT "Children of Joseph and Sarah (Clarke) Benjamin:
1 CONT "30. Sarah Benjamin bc. 1667 at Yarmouth? she m. Nov. 31, 1708 as the 2nd wife of Thomas Knowlton. This child might very well have been born after John, since her marriage date is so late. However, she is placed thus in the listing of children in t
1 CONC he inventory, and in most other listings of the children of Joseph. 
1 CONT "*31. Hannah Benjamin b. Feb. 1668/9 at Yarmouth (vr). 
1 CONT "*32. Mary (Marah) Benjamin b. April 1670 at Yarmouth (vr) 
1 CONT "*33. Joseph Benjamin b. April 29, 1673/4 at Yarmouth. 
1 CONT "*34. Mercy (Marsy) Benjamin b. March 12, 1674/5 at Yarmouth (vr). 
1 CONT "*35. Elizabeth Benjamin b. Jan. 14, 1679 at Yarmouth (vr) 
1 CONT "*36. John Benjamin b. 1682 at Yarmouth (vr-too badly worn to read). 
1 CONT "37. Kesiah Benjamin b. 1688. In the Notes of Barnstable Families Vol. I, mention is made of a dau. Kesiah (p. 143), but Mrs. E. E. Rogers, when doing research for her manuscript could find no record of such a child born to Joseph. This might be th
1 CONC e above Sarah, or the name Kesiah is used quite often used as a nickname for Mary. 
1 CONT "38. Jerusha Benjamin. Likewise, Dr. Wilson included a dau. named Jerusha in his manuscript and what was published in the Grafton Magazine.  Because your compilier wishes to keep all avenues open to the researcher, they are included, tho no other r
1 CONC ecords have been obtained for them. 
1 CONT "Resource: p. 106 Savage's Gen. Dictionary of N.E. Settlers; p. 1090 Cutter's Gen, and Family Hist. of Conn.; Gen. of Park Benjamin by Hoover; Grafton Magazine of Hist, and Gen . Vol 1 and 2 p. 38; Wilson Papers; History of Westminister, Mass. by H
1 CONC eywood, 1893; N. E. Gen. and Hist. Register Vol. 2 p. 64, Vol. 7 p. 178, and Vol. 19 p. 169; Hartford Times Dec. 14, 1946; The Benjamin Family of Norwich and Preston by E. E. Rogers."
1 CONT 
