0 @N2804@ NOTE "The Tracy Genealogy" ( Sherman Weld Tracy... "The Tracy Genealogy" )
1 CONT 
1 CONT Pages 24-26   Image 28-30 of 267 - The known children of Stephen and Tryphosa (Lee), all (but the first), born in Plymouth
1 CONT 
1 CONT i.  Sarah, b. at Leyden, Hlland, about 1622 and d. about 1708.  She m. at Plymouth, MA 16 Nov 1638 George Partridge, who is said to have come from Kent,England, onthe 'James.'  He d. about 1695.  Inventory of his estate was taken 16 Oct 1695.  He a
1 CONC s constable, juryman, surveyor of highways in Duxbury.  Served in Captain Standish's company.  (Partridge Genealogy, by George Henry Partridge, Minneapolis, MN, privately printed in 1915; data furnished by Mr. E.V.D. Seldn, OIl City, Pa., a descend
1 CONC ant of Triphosa (Partridge) West)
1 CONT 
1 CONT ii.  Rebecca, b. between 1624 and 1627.  She m. at Duxbury about 1642, William Meyrick (Merrick) later of Eastham.  This is shown by an Old Colony record in which a Power of Attorney date 2 July 1686 is given by William Merrick and Rebecca, his wif
1 CONC e to my trusty and well beloved Kinsman, John Partridge of Duxborough."  John Partridge was the son of George Partridge who married Sara Tracy.  (Merrick Genealogy; Old Colony Records)
1 CONT 
1 CONT iii.  Ruth, b. 1629; living 1655, mentioned by father in will
1 CONT 
1 CONT iv. Mary, living 1655, mentioned by father in will.
1 CONT 
1 CONT v. John-2 Tracy, b. 1633 at what became Duxbury, MA; died at Windham, CT, 30 May 1718.   Married Mary Prence, daughter of Governor Thomas Prence and his second wife Mary Collier.  John was proposed as a freeman in June 1656 and was admitted as suc
1 CONC h in June 1657.  In 1658 he was a constable of Duxbury and he served many times as grand juryman, as arbitrator and as town surveyor..Having served forst as an ensign in the militia he was  elected lieutenant in 1684; was a selectman continuously f
1 CONC rom 1677 to 1694 and colony representative from Duxbury in 1683, 1684 and 1686.  During his residence in Duxbury he was usally referred to as Ensign John Tracy.  In January 1694-95 he deeded to his son John land near Jones River which was probabl
1 CONC y the original Stephen Tracy homestead.....In 1701 he certified to surveying certain land and that year in July the selectmen chose Ensign John with two others to notify the minister of his election.  At the same meeting twenty acres of land was gr
1 CONC anted to Deborah (____) Tracy, widow of John Tracy deceased.  On 25 March 1704 John deeded land at Prince's Bottom at Plymouth to Nathaniel Warren and about that time he removed to Windham, CT, to live with his son Stephen.
