0 @N2833@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT For more information about Roger's christening record, please see article in the New England Historical and Genealogical Register, Spring 2017 by Rachelle Child and Helen Schatvet Ullmann, which proves he was christened 25 July 1620 in Prescot, Lan
1 CONC cashire, England.   
1 CONT The next place we find Roger is Dorchester, which was founded in 1630, and was annexed to Boston in 1870. 
1 CONT Roger first appears in the church records (see below) as a member June 9, 1640.  In this record, his name is spelled "Rodger Billindge."  On 8 Mar 1644 his [apparent] wife Mary appears on church as records as "Goody Billindge."  
1 CONT The source for birth information for Roger's children include the civil vital records of Dorchester, which can be found on ancestry.com, and a collection of the "Records of the First Church at Dorchester ... 1636-1734" which can be found online. Th
1 CONC ere are 7 children I believe belong to Roger and his first wife Mary:
1 CONT 1. Joseph Billings, for whom no baptism or birth record apparently exists, but who is mentioned in his father's will.  In a land deed dated 1694 his brothers Ebenezer and Roger conveyed a piece of property to his son Joseph Billings of Boston, tayl
1 CONC or, only son of Joseph Billings, late of Boston, mariner, eldest son of Roger Billings... 
1 CONT 2. The first Mary. This first Mary was born 10 July 1643 and died Dec. 4 1643. Her birth record can be found in Dorcester vital records and indicates she was the daughter of Roger and Mary ("Mary, the daughter of Roger Billing & Mary his wife was b
1 CONC orn the 10th (5th) 1643; and died the 4th (10th) 1643." 
1 CONT 3. Anna, whose christening record can be found in the church records described above - christened Oct. 20 1644 (see page 157).  Her name appears to be spelled "Anna Billindge."  Later in her marriage record her first name was spelled Hannah.  
1 CONT *4. A second Mary who was christened 23 November 1645 (see page 158) "Mary Billindge"
1 CONT 5. Jonathan, who was christened Aug. 25 1650 (see page 160) "Jonathan Billindge"
1 CONT 6. Ebenezer, who was christened Oct. 26, 1651 (see page 161) "Ebinezer Billindge" and presumably died before next Ebenezer was born
1 CONT 7. Samuel, who was christened 26 Oct, 1651 (see page 161) "Samuell Billindge" who was not mentioned in his father's will, probably died before 1683 
1 CONT Some have speculated that perhaps Mary died giving birth to these twins in 1651, due to the fact that there is a gap now in births (from 1651 to 1656) and due to the fact that a woman named Hannah Billings was admitted to the church in 1655.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT And there are several children who we believe belong to Roger and his second wife Hannah. Hannah was admitted to Dorchester church October 14, 1655 (page 20, her name was spelled "Hannah Billinge") and died May 25, 1662.
1 CONT 8. Ebenezer, christened Jan. 11, 1655/6 (see page 167) "Ebinezer Billindge"
1 CONT 9. Roger, who has both a birth record and a christening record (born Nov. 18 1657, bapt. Dec. 13 1657, see page 168) "Rodger ye sone of Rodger"
1 CONT 10. Elizabeth, who has both a birth record and a christening record (born Oct. 27 1659, bapt. Nov. 27 1659, see page 170) "Elizabeth ye daughter of Rodger Belleng"
1 CONT 11. Zipporah, who has both a birth record and a christening record (born May 21 1662, bapt. June 22 1662, see page 172). Her mother Hannah died at her birth. "Zipporah ye daughter of Roger Belleng was baptized ye 22 (4) 62 being about a month old
1 CONC : at ye birth of whom her mother dyed"
1 CONT 
1 CONT Shortly after the death of Hannah, Roger married third Elizabeth Pratt, possibly the daughter of John Pratt.  See the above-mentioned "Records of the First Church..." p. 155, where Elizabeth Pratt is baptized [apparently as an adult] into the churc
1 CONC h at Dorchester 19 February 1642/[3?].  It says there that she was "married to Roger Billings." 
1 CONT Roger is mentioned in "History of the town of Dorchester, Massachusetts Number two. Chapter IX. Second emigration from England," see page 110. According to this source, "Roger Billings joined the church in 1640, and was admitted freeman in 1643. Wi
1 CONC th John Gill he bought a hundred acres of land from the top of Milton Hill ... in 1656. He afterwards removed to Mr. Glover's farm, near Squantum, where he died in 1683. His first wife Mary, and by her he had a daughter, born 10 (5) 1643, and proba
1 CONC bly a son Joseph, whom he mentions in his will. By his second wife Hannah he had - Mary, baptized 23 (9) 1645, who married Samuel Belcher, 15 (10) 1663; Hannah, married John Penniman, 24 (12) 1664; Ebenezer and Samuel, baptized 26 (8) 1651; Roger
1 CONC , born 18 (9) 1657; Elizabeth, 27 (8) 1659, probably married Nathaniel Wales; Zipora, born 21 (30 1662, died October 8, 1676; Jonathan, died January 14, 1677. His wife Hannah died 25 (3) 1663." 
1 CONT I think that the specifics of which child belong to which mother in the above paragraph may be incorrect. His wife Hannah was admitted to the church in Dorchester October 14, 1655 (see page 20 of the above-mentioned church records). Only the civi
1 CONC l record for 1643 appears to mention any of the mother's names for any of the children (the first Mary was the daughter of Mary according to the civil records), so there is some educated guess work. 
1 CONT The town of Dorchester began as an independent town.  Many pieces of it were split off to form other towns, and eventually it was annexed by Boston.  The town of Milton was settled in 1640 as part of Dorchester and was made an independent town in 1
1 CONC 662.  In a book about the history of Milton, entitled "The History of Milton, Mass., 1640 to 1887," the last name of "Billings" is mentioned many times, often in relations to Billings Tavern.  Sources say it was built prior to 1681 and was the hom
1 CONC e of Roger in his last years.  "Farther south, on Canton avenue, at the top of the hill, and directly in front of the Wainwright mansion, stood the Billings tavern, in the early part of this century, called the "Blue Hill Tavern," an ancient hostel
1 CONC ry, and a famed resort for fancy dinners and high living....In this house was born Charles Howland Hammatt Billings, the distinguished architect. He was son of Ebenezer Billings. Joseph Billings, son of the Joseph who first kept the tavern, was a l
1 CONC eather-dresser."  On page 127 of this book, it has the following to say: "John Gill was a member of the Dorchester Church in 1640.  He was lessee with Roger Billings of John Glover's "Newbury farm" at Squantum in 1641.  He married Ann Billings, sis
1 CONC ter of Roger."  Squantum was part of Dorchester before joining Quincy when it was incorporated as a town separate from Braintree in 1792. 
1 CONT I am not sure if this book regarding the history of Milton and the book regarding the history of Dorchester agree completely on where Roger lived and when, but his name is associated with Dorchester, Milton, and an area that eventually became Quinc
1 CONC y.  His headstone is in the Hancock Cemetery which is now located in Quincy. His death and his funeral are mentioned in the above-referenced book about the history of Milton on page 653.  
1 CONT Hancock Cemetery is a historic cemetery on Hancock Street in Quincy Square, across the street from the United First Parish Church in Quincy, Massachusetts.  
1 CONT There is a good article about Roger entitled "Roger Billings of Milton, Mass., and some of His Descendants," by Harold Ward Dana, published in 1938 and 1939.  More recently, there is a good book published by Helen Schatvet Ullman, which was based o
1 CONC n the earlier article by Dana but corrected a few smaller mistakes.  Since this book is copyrighted (but can be accessed at the main Family History Library in SLC online) I will highlight a few facts here:  
1 CONT 1.  Helen indicates that while Roger owned land in Milton, he actually lived in Dorchester.  Roger's residence was on or near Squantum's Neck, which was part of Dorchester as of 1641 but much later became part of Quincy. 
1 CONT 2.  Dana omitted the second Ebenezer in his article.  This Ebenezer married Hannah Wales.  
1 CONT 3.  It appears that Roger did indeed have a sister named Ann.  In her will dated 16 July 1683 she made "my loveing brother Roger Billings Sen of Dorchester" her executor. 
1 CONT 4.  By 6 April 1653 Roger was living on the Glover Farm (earlier called the Newbury Farm) in what is now North Quincy. Quincy is now part of Norfolk County, which was created in 1793.  
1 CONT 5.  In January 1656/7? Roger & John Gill purchased 100 acres from the top of Milton Hill. 
1 CONT 6.  At the time he wrote his will he was living at Glover's Farm.  Roger's wrote a will and a codicil, the latter of which was dated November 13, 1683.  His wife was mentioned in his will, but not by name.  
1 CONT 7.  Helen refutes the groundless claim that Roger was born in Taunton, England, which has unfortunately been perpetuated on the internet and through actual fraud.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT Roger had 11 children, and only 3 of them outlived him.  He lived to see the death of 8 of his children and two wives.  He must have been acquainted with sorrow. It appears that Elizabeth and Zipporah died within a few days of each other, and Roge
1 CONC r and Ebinezer did too.   
1 CONT Roger's daughter Mary married into the Belcher family. So did his niece, Rebecca Gill.
