1 CONT There was additional historical information from a previous contributor that unfortunately vanished.  Much of it will be repeated in the source link above.
0 @N2839@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT From the "Great Migration" book by Robert Charles Anderson, which are highly reliable summaries of early pre-1636 colonists:
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Jeffrey Ferris
1 CONT 
1 CONT Origin: Unknown.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Migration: 1634 
1 CONT 
1 CONT First Residence: Watertown
1 CONT 
1 CONT Removes: Wethersfield 1635, Stamford 1641, Greenwich by 1650, East Town 1655, Greenwich by about 1659.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Church Membership: Admission to Watertown church prior to 6 May 1635 implied by freemanship.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Freeman: 6 May 1635 (second in a sequence of eight Watertown men) [MBCR 1:370].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Education: Signed his will by mark. His inventory included "one Bible [and] 2 psalm books" valued at 6s.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Offices: Connecticut petit jury, 1639 [CCCR 1:29]. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT His inventory included "a gun barrel" valued at 5s.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Estate: The estate of John Oldham owed £3 15s. to "Jeffery Ferris," 6 February 1639/40 [CCCR 1:43].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 26 [May?] 1641, "the lands of Jeffery Ferris lying in Wethersfeild" consisted of eight parcels: "one piece whereon his house standeth containing four acres"; "one piece in the great meadow containing three acres"; "one other piece also in the gr
1 CONC eat meadow containing seven acres"; "one piece in the west side of middle field in the dry swamp containing ten acres"; "one piece on the east side of Conecticutt River containing forty & five acres"; "one piece in pennie wise containing four acres
1 CONC "; "one piece lying in the west field containing thirteen acres"; and "one piece also in the west field containing thirteen acres" [WetLR 1:189].
1 CONT 
1 CONT In a list of "acres of marsh and upland allowed" at Stamford in 1641 "Jeff[rey] Firries" had ten acres [TAG 10:41, citing Stamford TR 1:6].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 25 November 1650, "William Hallet of Grenwich" sold to "Jeffere Ferris & his heirs, all his housing & lands in Grenwich, his whole right & title, & his wife's right also, in the lands purchased by Daniell Pattrick & Robert Feke in the New Nether
1 CONC lands, the parcels of lands that William Hallet doth own" are a house lot, "a piece of salt meadow of five acres more or less, also upland by the side of it on Elizabeth Neck, also the half of the land in the said Elizabeth Neck that is not alread
1 CONC y given out, both upland & meadow, also six acres of meadow, more or less, with a piece of upland by it ..., also a piece of upland in consideration of the ox pasture Goodman Sherwood did & should take in, ... also a piece of upland & meadow on Mya
1 CONC nos Neck that was William Hallett his particular lot" [Greenwich LR 1:169]. (On 18 July 1640, Robert Feake and Daniel Patrick purchased from several Indians land that would become Greenwich; an annotation of unknown date to this deed states that "K
1 CONC eofrum hath sold all his right in the abovesaid neck unto Jeffere Ferris [Greenwich LR 1:455].)
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 28 May 1661, "Jeffery Ferries, by marriage with his wife Susanna, now deceased, stands engaged to pay certain legacies due to the children of Robert Lockwood, deceased, according to the administration entered in Court 20 October 1658" [Fairfiel
1 CONC d PR 1:65].
1 CONT 
1 CONT In his will, dated 6 January 1664(/5?] and proved 9 March 1666/7, "Jeaffrey Ferris" bequeathed to "my wife that now is, all the estate which she can make appear that she brought with her" and £l0; to "her four children, that is to say, those fou
1 CONC r boys which I brought up and kept," £10 apiece; "in case my wife that now is shall see cause to remain a widow alter my decease, ... I give for her use five acres of land that now is in till, the one half in my home lot and the other half at Tomu
1 CONC ck," also "one of my allotments that is on Mihanoes Neck," also some household goods to "my son James Ferris," £20, some moveables, and "half my farm, that is to say, that land that I now gave him"; to "my daughter Mary Lockwood, that now is, twen
1 CONC ty pounds besides the quarter part of my before mentioned jades I gave to her husband, Jonothan Lockwood"; to "my son Peter Ferris, his three children, one mare colt"; to "my son Josep[h]'s two children one mare colt"; residue to "be equally divide
1 CONC d amongst all my children"; "in case God should please to give my wife a child born of her body whilst she remains my wife, that is to say, whilst I live, or if I leave her with child when I die," that child to have "the other half of my farm" an
1 CONC d a young mare; "my loving friend, John Holly, now in Stamford, and my son Peter Ferris" to be executors [Fairfield PR 2:20-21].
1 CONT 
1 CONT The inventory of the estate of "Jeffrey Ferris, deceased," taken 23, 24, 26 and 29 November 1666, totalled £493 6-¼d., of which £170 was real estate: "house and lands," £170 [Fairfield PR 2:29-32]. Appended to the inventory is a division of th
1 CONC e estate, dated 22 March 1666/7: to "brother Jonathan Lockwood"; to "brother James Ferris"; to "brother Joseph Ferris"; and to "brother Joseph Ferris" [Fairfield PR 2:32] (perhaps one of the entries for Joseph Ferris is intended for one of the othe
1 CONC r brothers).
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 21 June 1687, "Peter Ferris & Jonathan Lockwood & Joseph Ferris & Jeames Ferris, being administrators of the estate of our deceased father, Jeffere Ferris," agreed "that all lands that are dividable that were formerly our foresaid deceased fathe
1 CONC r's, ... that any or each person of us do possess, that he or they shall have ... the said land ... as his own proper right" [Greenwich LR 1:124].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 16 July 1705, "John Ferris, inhabitant of Westchester, ... son unto Jeffre Ferris of Greenwich," gave a receipt for his share of his father's estate from "my brother[s] Peeter Ferris & Joseph Feris & James Feris & Jonathan Lockwood, ... as the
1 CONC y were administrators of the estate of my deceased [father] Jeffere Feris" [Greenwich LR 1:173].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Birth: By about 1604 based on estimated date of marriage.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Death: Greenwich 31 May 1666 [Gillespie Anc 118, source not cited].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Marriage: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT (1) By about 1629 ___ ___. She died at Stamford 31 July 1658 (TAG 10:45, citing Stamford TR 1:20].
1 CONT 
1 CONT (2) About 1659 Susanna (Norman) Lockwood, daughter of Richard Norman {1626, Salem} [GMB 2:1334-36] and widow of Robert Lockwood {1634, Watertown} [Fairfield PR 1:65]. She died at Greenwich 23 December 1660 (recorded at Stamford) [TAG 10:118, citin
1 CONC g Stamford TR 1:98].
1 CONT 
1 CONT (3) By about 1662 Judith (Feake) Palmer, daughter of James Feake of London and widow of William Palmer {1636, Watertown} [NYGBR 86:211-12, and arguments presented there]. She married (3) John Bowers soon after the death of Jeffrey Ferris, for, o
1 CONC n 9 March 1666/7, "Judah Bowers, lately widow Ferris sometimes wife to Jeffery Ferris, do openly acknowledge I have received in full satisfaction what was given me by my husband's will" [Fairfield PR 2:21].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Children:
1 CONT 
1 CONT With first wife
1 CONT 
1 CONT i. Peter, b. say 1629 in. (1) Stamford 15 July 1654 Elizabeth Reynolds [Stamford TR 1:55]; m. (2) Stamford 25 July 1705 Ruth (___) Weed [TAG 11:91, citing Stamford TR 1:128], widow of Daniel Weed [Gillespie Anc 537-38], son of Jonas Weed {1630, Wat
1 CONC ertown} [GMB 3:1956-59].
1 CONT 
1 CONT ii. Joseph, b. say 1632; in Stamford 20 November 1657 Ruth Knapp [TAG 10:112, citing Stamford TR 1:74], daughter of Nicholas Knapp {1630, Watertown) [GMB 2:1136].
1 CONT 
1 CONT iii. Mary, b. say 1636; m. (1) by an unknown date Jonathan Lockwood, son of Robert Lockwood {1634, Watertown); m. (2) soon after 3 June 1696 Thomas Merritt [Gillespie Anc 128, citing prenuptial agreement, location not stated].
1 CONT 
1 CONT iv. John, b. about 1640 (deposed 5 December 1705 aged 65 years old (Gillespie Anc 128, citing an unknown source]); m. (1) by about 1666 Mary Jackson (eldest known child m. in 1686 (Gillespie Anc 128-29, and arguments presented there]; m. (2) Grac
1 CONC e ____ [Gillespie Anc 128].
1 CONT 
1 CONT v. James, b. say 1642; m. by about 1670 ___ ___ [TAG 26:230-32 (which was apparently not seen by Prindle [Gillespie Anc 129], with whom we do not concur)].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Comments: On 6 February 1639/40, "Jeffery Ferris" was fined for various misdemeanors in the case of Mr. Plum and Richard Westcott [CCCR 1:44].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jeffrey Ferris was in East Town on 1 January 1657, when he signed a declaration [Gillespie Anc 124], and was of East Town when he prosecuted a lengthy lawsuit in late 1658 and early 1659 [Westchester Court 11-15].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Bibliographic Note: In 1976 Paul W. Prindle prepared a comprehensive account of this immigrant and his family [Gillespie Anc 117-36]." "The New York Genealogical and Biographical Record," 86(1955):132-148, 209-221, "The Feake Family of Norfolk, Lon
1 CONC don, and Colonial America," by George E. McCracken.
