0 @N2942@ NOTE YORK, JOHN.
1 CONT 	SAMUEL.
1 CONT 
1 CONT John was probably son of John and Ruth (Graves). John, Sr., was
1 CONT killed in the attack as John, Jr., was said to have been, but
1 CONT Mrs. Swarton wrote that when the captives were separated she and
1 CONT "One John York" were the only two at Norridgewock, and that they
1 CONT "were both, almost starv'd for want." Their masters told them if
1 CONT they "could not hold up" they would kill them. "And accordingly
1 CONT John York, growing weak by his wants, they killed him."
1 CONT 
1 CONT Samuel.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The carpenter of the following memorial may have been brother of
1 CONT John who was killed. His wife was Hannah and they lived in what
1 CONT is now Topsham. Their son Samuel testified in 1726 that their
1 CONT house was made a garrison where several other families were
1 CONT entertained until driven off by Indians. York Ledge and York
1 CONT Landing of Falmouth Foreside recall this family. Samuel, Sr.,
1 CONT must have returned, as householder or soldier as shown by the
1 CONT "Memorial of Samuel York, Carpenter addressed to his Excellency
1 CONT the Earle of Bellomont at Albany the 2d of Septr 1700 sheweth:
1 CONT That I was taken prisoner in Casco Bay... in the moneth of May
1 CONT 1690 and carried to Canada where and in
1 CONT 
1 CONT ¹ "Que. Docs., III, 6.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 209
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT the hunting Indian countries I have lived ever since till the
1 CONT 29th day of this last July that I made my escape to come hither
1 CONT and during the last two years & half I was imployed in cutting
1 CONT masts for the use of the French King's Navy."
1 CONT 
1 CONT He gives the governor much information about the Western
1 CONT Indians; says that they and several bush-rangers who are with
1 CONT them are discontented and wish to come and trade with the
1 CONT English.¹ Before he returned to Casco Lord Bellomont sent him
1 CONT and others with a message to the Dowaganhas Indians, but they
1 CONT were stopped by "Indians of the Five Nations, who refused to
1 CONT accompany them and who told them (York and his friends) to
1 CONT return to Albany unless they meant to be knocked in the head by
1 CONT the French or their Indians."² Samuel York died in March, 1718.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ¹N. Y. Docs., IV, 748.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ²N. Y. Docs., IV, 768.
0 @N2943@ NOTE Will & other mentions of the family in early records, including land transactions
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 220: John and Samuel (sons of first Richard); "James Thomas & Sam York"
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 221: James Thomas & Samuel purchase of land from indigenous Mainers in what is now Topsham; Samuel sells 1/2 of sawmill in Cape Porpoise in 1682
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 222: Samuel's lot near Mussel Cove in Casco Bay; "York Ledge" and "York's Landing" in Falmouth Foreside were still on the US Coast Survey map at the time of publication; legal disputes over Topsham land
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 223: records indicate that Richard  "had finally fixed upon his home in Yarmouth, where his brother John had become a most influential man"; John York, "the other brother," lived for awhile in Scarborough
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 224: John York in N Yarmouth; John killed 17 May 1690 at fall of Casco Fort
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 225: John York's children Richard, Benjamin, and Ruth (who later married Haskell); Richard & Benjamin "were probably of that devoted band of the flower of our youth, who ... were cut off to a man, during the siege of Casco fort."
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 226: Joseph, John's 4th & only remaining son
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 227: trying to figure how many children   were in this generation of Yorks (which, surprisingly, involves the use of fractions): "Thus it is proven both mathematically & genealogically, that John left 1 son and 1 daughter; and Joseph had 6 dau
1 CONC ghers as surviving heirs in 1735."
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 227: Benjamin b 1680; m Mary Giddings (1704); Benjamin lived in Falmouth, was deacon of the church, built a mill in Cape Elizabeth (p 228)
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 229: Deacon Samuel of N. Yarmouth was industrious & gave in charity "what amounted to quite a fortune for those days."
1 CONT 
1 CONT page 229: William Ring served in the Rev War; became a ship-builder in Falmouth Foreside; his sons all went to sea & all daughters but 1 married seafaring men.
