1 CONT From Wikipedia (for now...)
1 CONT 
1 CONT William Carpenter (c. 1610 – 7 September 1685) was an original settler of Rhode Island alongside Roger Williams and 14 others and Providence Plantations, born about 1610, probably in Amesbury, Wiltshire, England (home of Stonehenge). He died Se
1 CONC ptember 7, 1685, in the Pawtuxet section of Providence, now in Cranston, Rhode Island. He was listed by 1655 as a "freeman" of the colony.
1 CONT 
1 CONT William Carpenter was the son of Richard Carpenter, who was born in England, probably in or near the Wiltshire town and parish of Amesbury or the adjacent parish of Newton Ton(e)y. His mother may have been Alice Knight, but this is not confirmed.
1 CONT 
1 CONT William married Elizabeth Arnold (Nov 23, 1611 - after Sep 7, 1685), who was born in Ilchester, Somerset, England, the daughter of William Arnold (June 24, 1587 - 1675/76) and sister of Benedict Arnold, the first governor of the Colony of Rhode Isl
1 CONC and and Providence Plantations.[1] William and Elizabeth had eight children together: Joseph, Lydia, Ephraim, Priscilla, Timothy, Silas, Benjamin, and William. The couple were probably buried on their homestead in present-day Cranston, Rhode Island
1 CONC .[2][3]
1 CONT 
1 CONT William Carpenter is the first person bearing the surname "Carpenter" to make permanent settlement in America.[4] He settled in Providence Plantation and was instrumental in its development as a Colony, holding many public offices.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Providence Plantation
1 CONT 
1 CONT William Carpenter was not one of the first six settlers of Providence Plantation with Roger Williams[5] in 1636, but he arrived early the next spring with seven others.[6] His name is listed in the first deed executed in the settlement by Roger Wil
1 CONC liams.[7][8] In 1640, his name appears with the names of 38 others on an agreement to form a government in Providence.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Carpenter built a block house on his property soon after settling there for defense against Indian attacks, the first in the colony. Many of the surviving Providence Plantation settlers gathered there for protection from an Indian attack during Kin
1 CONC g Philip's War, and their brave stand compelled the Indians to retreat. Carpenter's son William Jr. was killed in the attack, along with many other settlers.[9] During King Philip's War, the counsel of the most judicious inhabitants of the colony w
1 CONC as sought by the General Assembly, and Carpenter was one of 16 individuals named in this request.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Public Offices
1 CONT 
1 CONT William Carpenter was one of four appointed by Boston authorities "to keepe the peace in [Pawtuxet]," 1642[–1658?].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Commissioner (deputy?) for Providence to Rhode Island General Court Assembly
1 CONT 1657-1665, 1675, 1676, 1679.
1 CONT Appointed juror, General Court of Trials
1 CONT 1657/8 (but did not serve), 1661[/2], 1663, 1664; juror for Grand Inquest, 1658/9, 1663, 1665; then warden (magistrate) for General Court of Trials, 1660/1.
1 CONT Providence town meeting moderator
1 CONT June 1662, June 1665, September 1665, April 1666, September 1666, October 1670, December 1670, February 1670/1, April–September 1671.
1 CONT General assistant for Providence to Rhode Island General Assembly
1 CONT 1665-1672.
1 CONT Providence justice of the peace
1 CONT 1665/6, 1667, 1668 and officiated marriages from his office as an assistant for the Providence to the Rhode Island General Assembly from 1669-1671/72.
1 CONT Providence town councilman
