0 @N2993@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT After nearly 175 years of research, the maiden name and parentage of Rose, wife of John Waterbury (1621-1658) has finally been established:  she was the daughter of Roger Porter and his wife Alice Mulford of Long Sutton, Hampshire, England, as is a
1 CONC mply demonstrated by Roger Porter's 1654 Estate Record at Middlesex County, Massachusetts.  Roger Porter came to Watertown, Massachusetts Bay in 1638 as a widower with his four daughters, all named in the ship's passenger list.  This should finall
1 CONC y demolish the endlessly repeated claims that Rose was a Lockwood.  Meanwhile, as to Rose being the daughter of Gregory Taylor, Gregory Taylor was called the "father-in-law" of John Waterbury in Taylor's Estate Record at Stamford because in the 17t
1 CONC h century, "father-in-law" had the additional meaning of "step-father."  Gregory Taylor had married c.1631 Alice (not Achsa), widow of William Waterbury, making him John Waterbury's step-father.  This is very capably set out in the "Waterbury" sect
1 CONC ion of "The Great Migration Begins" by Robert Charles Anderson, and there is no reason why any would-be contributors to this site should be ignorant of that essential source.
1 CONT   It would be a wonderful thing if those Family Search contributors who are unaware of modern research on these families would simply take the trouble to educate themselves, before charging off to plug in "Rose Lockwood" or "Rose Taylor" as the wif
1 CONC e of John Waterbury (1621-1658), just because they found it on some tree at "Ancestry," or elsewhere on line.  It simply isn't so.
0 @N2994@ NOTE No one seems to know who Mary's parents are. The Cogswells in America by E. O. Jameson, 1884, p.9 shows the children of William Cogswell and Susanna Hawkes. This Mary is not included.
1 CONT Thus The Descendants of William and Elizabeth Tuttle and Cushing and Allied Families are both not correct in claiming that this Mary is William Cogswell's daughter.
