0 @N3030@ NOTE Titles belong in Title Box - Titles are important genealogical information.  Can we please put an end to this false and bizarre premise that the title box should not include title information?  Yes, you may have been told that it's incorrec
1 CONC t to put a title in the title box unless the person was born with a title.  So, it exists exclusively for certain royal heirs? That's ridiculous.  This is not a data entry exercise.  We are supposed to be creating an accurate genealogical record. G
1 CONC enealogists are tasked with identifying people and titles are a part of that identification, especially military titles, but also titles and suffixes such as Dr., Rev., Phd.  It opens up the research and narrows the identification: Captain Christop
1 CONC her Avery will discern one Christopher from another, it will point to possible military, pension, land, cemetery and tax records, it will narrow down an age range, etc.  Why wouldn't you include a title? Is this a subtle effort to erase the honor a
1 CONC ssociated with military service or the accomplishments of higher education?  Do not disrespect your own good work and the standards of good genealogical research by removing honorable and well-earned titles because someone somewhere for some unknow
1 CONC n reason tells you not to.  KM
1 CONT 
1 CONT As already outlined above, the following is wholly incorrect.  Titles belong in the title box, titles are vital genealogical information, and earning a title does not automatically subordinate your name, it, in fact, elevates your name.   But don'
1 CONC t take my word for it; read any respected genealogical journal and you will not find titles in the footnotes as "alternate names."
1 CONT 
1 CONT >>You are partially correct.  Most people are not born with a title, they earn it.  As such it should be noted as an alternate name as I have changed it. to.  Note, most of the vital record also exclude his title as well.
