0 @N3040@ NOTE He moved to Salem in 1635 where he received a home lot in town and 300 acres of farmland south of Felton Hill "in the place where his cattle are by Brooksby."
0 @N3041@ NOTE By 1637 Robert Coles moved from Salem to Providence and in 1638 he became one of Providence's first 13 proprietors and a founding member of the first Baptist church congregation in America.[34][35] At the inaugural church meeting, at leas
1 CONC t twelve settlers gathered together with Roger Williams who, after being baptized by Ezekiel Holliman, baptized Coles and the others.[36]
0 @N3042@ NOTE Now Cranston
0 @N3043@ NOTE Coles, Chad Brown, William Harris, and John Warner co-authored the Providence Combination of 1640 containing proposals for a new form of government and referred to as the Combination. It was signed by 39 male and female townsmen—an earl
1 CONC y milestone in women's rights.[51] The Combination is listed among the colonial documents that influenced American constitutionalism.[52]
1 CONT 
1 CONT The Combination replaced the direct democracy of the original compact of 1637 with a representative, democratic government designed to solve disputes, especially land disputes.[53] It contained 12 articles that defined the borders of Providence, cr
1 CONC eated an elected board of arbitrators and an appeals process, created town offices, and affirmed the separation of church and state as the determination "to hold forth liberty of conscience." The Combination resolved the problem of assembling a quo
1 CONC rum of busy townsmen to make decisions.[54][55] The General Assembly replaced the arbitration system with a town charter in 1649
0 @N3044@ NOTE Previous to 1639, the Pawtuxet sachem Socononoco sold land to Roger Williams that stretched from the meadows on the Pawtuxet River southward to Conimicut Point.[44][45] On January 1, 1639, Williams sold a share to Coles that included an inl
1 CONC and meadow and land at Passeonkquis Cove and Namquid, now called Gaspee Point, and Coles was granted permission by the Pawtuxet tribe to graze cattle on their farmland in the winter. On February 10, 1641, Coles received a confirmatory deed from th
1 CONC e sachems Canonicus and Miantonomi.[46]
1 CONT 
1 CONT By 1648—the year Shawomet was renamed to honor the Earl of Warwick—Coles was listed as a townsman of Warwick, where he was a mill proprietor and resided for the remainder of his life
