0 @N3099@ NOTE A picture of this document dated 1 July 1692 is attached in the Memories section.  PLEASE READ THE FIRST 3 LINES WHERE IT STATES: "William Cash & Rebeckah his wife of Westmoreland Co."  This couple DID NOT LIVE IN CHARLES COUNTY. They wer
1 CONC e selling land most likely inherited from Rebeckah's father. Rebeckah most likely grew up in Charles County, MD. if you take a minute to look at a map, Charles County is DIRECTLY ACROSS THE POTOMAC RIVER FROM WESTMORELAND COUNTY--A SHORT BOAT RID
1 CONC E AWAY.
0 @N3100@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT WILLIAM CASH, the progenitor of the southern branch of the CASH Family, first appears in the records of Westmoreland County, Virginia, 30 May 1694, on which date he was sworn as a grand juror.  It is believed he came from England ca 1680, and is th
1 CONC erefore referred to by most CASH researchers as "The Emigrant". His date of birth is estimated as ca 1660. His plantation was situated in Washington Parish, Westmoreland County, but when King George County was formed from part of Westmoreland in 17
1 CONC 20, approximately half of this land fell into each county. His first wife is unknown (she is identified - see later). His first wife probably died ca 1698 and he was then married ca 1699 to Elizabeth, believed to have been the daughter of his frien
1 CONC d and neighbor John SKINNER, a Quaker. He died in Westmoreland County, Virginia between 16 February 1708, the date of his Will, and 25 August 1708, the date of probate.  {Find A Grave is often just WRONG and should never be considered  "source" wit
1 CONC hout additional verification.} 
1 CONT A deed from Charles County, Maryland, Liber 4, p.432, plainly names the likely first wife wife for William Cash who had apparently died sometime after 1692. [FHL film #007737871, frame #674]
1 CONT 1 Jul 1692.  William Cash and Rebeckah his of wife of Westmoreland Co, VA sold 25 acres to Henry Key of Charles County, MD.  It was part of one hundred acres of land in possession of Thomas Maris at his decease and now belongs to the four daughter
1 CONC s of Thomas Maris.  (Rebeckah was surely one of these daughters.)  signed William Cash, by his "W" mark; Rebeckah Cash by her "X" mark.  witnesses were Thomas "T" Liloard and Mary "M" Boughton.
1 CONT William Cash & his wife Rebeckah appointed William Stone to be their lawful attorney to acknowledge this deed.
1 CONT Witnesses attested to deed on 9 Aug 1692.
1 CONT Another version found for William Cash: "William Cash, born about 1653, settled in Westmoreland, Colony of Virginia. He died in Washington Parish, Westmoreland, Colony of Virginia in 1708. He married about 1677. “ A British tax record in 1677, li
1 CONC sts William Cash as "seated" in Washington Parish, Westmoreland. 
1 CONT He left a will, written 16 February 1708/7, and recorded in Westmoreland County, 25 August 1708, naming his children: William, Thomas, John, James, Howard, Joseph, Peter, and a daughter, Anise Cash. His wife, Elizabeth was named as executrix and gu
1 CONC ardian of his children, William, Thomas, Anise, until they became of age. Therefore it is believed all the other children were older than these three who were not yet of age as stated in the will. Peter Skinner was named as executor, and John Skinn
1 CONC er a witness - which has led people to speculate Elizabeth's surname was Skinner."
1 CONT 
1 CONT Notes for WILLIAM CASH: Although there is a grave marker stating his birth place as "Scotland" - it is a recent marker not placed anywhere near the time of his death. There is NO actual record or documentation found anywhere proving his place of bi
1 CONC rth.
0 @N3101@ NOTE Who was the mother of the children of William Cash?
1 CONT Rebeckah is named as his wife in a deed in 1692 - so at least some of the children born prior to that date were hers. She has no known death date, nor is there any known marriage record to Elizabeth Skinner. Some of the children could have been her
1 CONC s. Elizabeth, second wife, was appointed guardian of the youngest children but they were born about 1689, 1693, and 1697.
