0 @N3156@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Richard Babson, born in Gloucester, June 1, 1663; married (1) about 1686, Mary Dolliver, who died in Gloucester, Feb. 14, 1717/18, aged about 54 years. He married (2) in Gloucester, Oct. 14, 1718, Jane Reading, widow of John Reading who died in Glo
1 CONC ucester, Nov. 17, 1716, aged about 56 years. They lived at Fresh Water Cove. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT On Oct. 10, 1720, he sold to his son John Babson his house and land at Fresh Water Cove for £34 and acknowledged the deed at 
1 CONT Falmouth, Me. He styled himself seaman or coaster and may have removed to Falmouth about 1720. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT «b»Children, born in Gloucester: «/b»
1 CONT 1.) John, b. July 9, 1687. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 2.) Sarah, b. Jan. 12, 1689; m. Feb. 3, 1709, Nathan Millet who was drowned near Manchester Neck, Jan. 6, 1724, leaving a widow and seven children. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 3.) Joseph, b. June 25, 1690; d. Feb. 1, 1690/91. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 4.) Joseph, 2d, b. Nov. 19, 1691; d. July 19, 1692. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 6.) Hannah, b. Dec. 22, 1695; m. Dec. 23, 1714, James Sawyer, Jr. and settled near her father's home at Fresh Water Cove. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 6.) Experience, b. Apr. 23, 1698. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 7.) Rebecca, b. Aug. 6, 1700. She had a son called James Dyke, b. March 12, 1720. "Tis reported that his mother dyed at Billerica 
1 CONT about Apr. 8, 1720, aged 19.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 8.) Eunice, b. Mar. 14,1703; m. Dec. 24,1723, John Millet, of Gloucester. They lived on the Rev. Richard Blynman farm at Kettle Cove. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 9.) Thomas, b. May 3, 1706; d. May 26, 1706. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT 10.) Abigail, b. May 13, 1708; probably m. Mar. 28, 1727, Job Denning.
1 CONT 
1 CONT He was granted "six acres of upland ground at the cape and it fell out to by Lott to be the Twenty first Lott in number" on 27 Feb 1687/88 at Gloucester. He was granted "one acre between the high hill and the highway that leads to Norman's Woe, no
1 CONC t to prejudice the way that comes out of the wood" at Gloucester. He was granted "4 acres of swampy ground above the Long Bridge" at Gloucester after 1688. He received a grant of land from his mother, Elinor Hill, in exchange for her maintenance o
1 CONC n 13 Aug 1695.
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 11 May 1696 at Gloucester Richard sold about a quarter acre of land to his brother-in-law, Thomas Witham, located adjacent to land Thomas had already received from his Mother-in-Law, Eleanor Babson.119 He sold John Smith, Senr, a common right i
1 CONC n Gloucester at Gloucester on 24 May 1697.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Richard Babson  sold lot #60 to John Smith, Senr on 9 Mar 1709 at Gloucester.
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 26 Aug 1712 Richard Babson  sold land at Ipswich to Jacob Davis on the south side of the road lading to Chebacco in Ipswich. He also sold him 12 acres on Chebacco side on the line between Gloucester and Manchester on 26 Aug 1712. He sold land a
1 CONC t Gloucester on 20 Jun 1715 "boatman, alias fisherman," mortgaged to Col. Samuel Brown, Esq. of Salem, on 20 June 1715, the homestead where he dwelled, consisting of a dwelling house and about 5 acres of land, bounded easterly by Fresh Water Cove
1 CONC , northeasterly by a Mill Brook, westerly by ye highway and easterly by Richard Dolliver's land, along with all the "fruit trees timber wood underwood rocks stones mines mineralls & all the privileges & appurtenances there unto belonging..." Richar
1 CONC d was to pay Samuel Brown "£32 current money ... at or before the fifteenth day of November next." There were clearly some extensions granted as the mortgage was not fully discharged until 16 April 1719.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Richard Babson discharged a mortgage on 16 Apr 1719.125 He was granted in Feb 1720 at Falmouth, ME. He sold to his son John on 5 Oct 1720 "all my houseing & Land Lying in Gloucester being Scituate in or near fresh water Cove in glocester ... Contai
1 CONC ning about one acre & halfe..." Richard and his wife Jane acknowledged this deed at Falmouth, York County the following day.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Richard Babson was granted additional land at at Falmouth, ME, on 13 Mar 1721.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Richard Babson appears on the muster roll as a "Centenll" between 1 Jun 1725 and 30 Jun 1725 at Capt. John Gray's company. He ended military service on 30 Nov 1725. He sold land at Falmouth, ME, on 29 Sep 1729 Richard sold to Moses Pearson, Joyner
1 CONC , for £28 all his right and interest in the Township of Falmouth, which is "equal to any one of the present Comoners or Inhabitants of sd Township" which he held by "Vertue of my being a Commoner of sd according to the Scheme for the Settling sd T
1 CONC own (Except my thirty Acre Lot which I have already disposed of...)."
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 11 May 1730 is the last date his name appears in the town meeting records at Falmouth, ME.
1 CONT _____________________________________________
1 CONT Richard Babson and his second wife, Jane, the widow of John Reading, probably moved to Falmouth, Maine, about 1720.  He settled at Fresh Water Cove where "he styled himself a seaman or coaster."  In October 1720 he sold his house and land at Fres
1 CONC h Water Cove to his son John.  All  of his children are from his first wife, Mary and all were born in Gloucester, Mass.
