0 @N3313@ NOTE The above date is inconsistent with his widow Joanna Westcott marrying in 1649 to Nathaniel Baldwin.  Could it be his son Richard's death instead?
0 @N3314@ NOTE "Joanna Skidmore seems to have died soon after [her will] was signed in June 1667. She was attended in her last illness by Governor Winthrop, Junior, who was in addition to his other talents the most sought after physician in New England. S
1 CONC till preserved is his case book in which he made brief notes on his patients and prescriptions. From this we find that at the time of her last illness Joanna was aged 55 and the elder sister of Ann Paine (who lived on until 1682) who had married Ro
1 CONC bert Sanford (1615-1676) of Hartford by 1643. Joanna and Ann Paine were the stepdaughters and not the daughters of Jeremy Adams (1604- 1683) as has frequently been said in error.." http://www.skidmorefamilyhistory.com/THOMAS%20SKIDMORE%20(SCUDAMORE
1 CONC ),%201605-1684,.pdf
0 @N3315@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Joanna's maiden name is unknown. (1667 - )
1 CONT I have attached under Memories a document by Warren Skidmore that can be found online at http://www.skidmoregenealogy.com/images/OccPap_no._20.pdf that discusses Joanna and her parentage, husbands, and children. To sum, it states that Joanna was ma
1 CONC rried first to Richard Westcott, with whom she had 4 children, and then to Nathaniel Baldwin, with whom she had an additional 3 children, and then finally to Thomas Skidmore. On 19 June 1667 Joanna Skidmore made a will at Fairfield; her sons John a
1 CONC nd Daniel Westcott were named executors of her will.  Joanna was attended at her last illness by Governor John Winthrop, Junior who was the "most sought after physician in New England, and her name is still preserved in his case book in which he ma
1 CONC de brief notes on his patients and prescriptions. From it we find that at the time of her last illness Joanna was age 55 and had a younger sister Ann who had married Robert Sanford...it was believed for a time that the sisters were the daughters o
1 CONC f Jeremy Adams...but a close attention to the records shows that Ann Sanford was not his daughter. The marriage in 1639 of Richard Westcott & Johan Pain (Johanna's first husband) was found in the register of the church at Braunton, Devon.  From thi
1 CONC s entry it was easy to identify Thomas Paine, of Milford, Connecticut, as the father of the sisters.  He is said elsewhere to have come from Devon, and the discovery can be confirmed as certain since Thomas Sanford, Junior... later married his cous
1 CONC in Elizabeth Paine."  
1 CONT  
1 CONT I am not sure if it can be entirely confirmed as certain, since several researchers said Richard Westcott was settled in Wethersfield by 1636 and bought Samuel Clark's homestead, so it is unlikely that he married in England in 1639.  This discrepan
1 CONC cy, in my opinion, sheds doubt on the hypothesis that Joanna's maiden name was Paine.    
1 CONT 
1 CONT The current city of Wethersfield Connecticut was founded in 1634 by a Puritan settlement party of "10 Men."  Wethersfield is arguably the oldest town in Connecticut.  In 1636-1637, some settlers of Westhersfield participated in a campaign against s
1 CONC ome local Indians known as the "Pequot War."  Richard is mentioned in the book, 'The History of Ancient Wethersfield, Connecticut,' on page 72, as one of the 26 men from Wethersfield who fought in this campaign.   Also, in the book, "History and Ge
1 CONC nealogy of the Families of Old Fairfield, Volume 1..." which can be found online, says on page 660 that "Richard Westcott settled in Wethersfield by 1636 and bought Samuel Clark's homestead; sold it to John Stoddard, 1645, and removed to Fairfield
1 CONC , where he died before 11 March 1649/50. So if Richard was in Connecticut by 1636, it is doubtful that he married in England in 1639.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT However, this article by Warren Skidmore does make a useful point in pointing out that her maiden name was not Adams.  The author writes on page 1, "It was believed for a time that the sisters [Joanna and Ann] were the daughters of Jeremy Adams (16
1 CONC 04 - 1683) but close attention to the records shows that Ann Sanford was not his daughter."  In footnote 3 the author writes that "Jeremy Adams died on 11 August 1683 at Hartford.  His will mentions a grandson Zachary Sanford, but he was a grandso
1 CONC n only by his marriage to Sarah Willett (a daughter of Sarah Adams by her husband Nathaniel Willett).  See Robert Charles Anderson, "The Great Migration Begins:  Immigrants to New England, 1620 - 1633 (Boston, 1995) I, 11.
1 CONT  
1 CONT The family of Joanna's second husband, Nathaniel Baldwin, is mentioned in some detail in Susan Woodruff Abbott's book, "Families of Early Milford, Connecticut" that can be found on ancestry.com. Nathaniel is mentioned in page 43, and says that he d
1 CONC ied in Fairfield, Conn., inventory of his estate taken 19 October 1658. He was a cooper (a maker of barrels and the like) and joined the church in Milford 9 June 1644, a freeplanter...He removed to Fairfield where he had lot #13 January 1653/4. H
1 CONC e married 1) Abigail Camp who died Milford 22 March 1648, probably sister of William Camp who was called Uncle by his son John when John made him executor of his estate - see LHHC-DXB below...He married 2) Joanne Westcott widow of Richard of Fairfi
1 CONC eld. He had 7 children. With first wife Abigail he had John (baptized 2 June 1644), Daniel (baptized 2 June 1644) Nathaniel (baptized January 1645/6 and Abigail (baptized 19 March 1648) and by second wife Joanne he had Sarah (b. 1650) Deborah (b. 1
1 CONC 652) and Samuel (born 1655).
1 CONT 
1 CONT The book, "History and Genealogy of the Families of Old Fairfield, Vol. I," which can be found in its entirety on ancestry.com, has an entry for Nathaniel starting on page 22. According to this source, inventory was taken on Nathaniel's estate 19 O
1 CONC ctober 1658. There are more details here about his estate and each of his children. It also has more details about Nathaniel's second wife, who remarried after his death. "The will of Joanna Skidmore, wife of Thomas, in 1667, named children John, D
1 CONC aniel, Joanna and Abigail Westcott, and Sarah, Deborah, and Samuel Baldwin. [Nathaniel's] second wife was Joan, widow of Richard Westcott; she m. (3) Thomas Skidmore. She was sister of the wife of Robert Sanford of Hartford."
1 CONT Another source, "The history of Fairfield : Fairfield County, Connecticut" which can also be found on ancestry.com, has, on page 65, an original map of the town of Fairfield, as well as the following: "Richard Westcoat, who had owned the house an
1 CONC d home-lot between John Gray's and John Nichol's, died soon after he settled at Fairfield. His widow married Nathaniel Baldwin, of Milford. Baldwin removed to Fairfield, and by virtue of this marriage occupied the dwellings and lands of Westcoat." 
1 CONT 
1 CONT According to some, the best account of Joanna's seven children can be found in Donald Lines Jacobus' book, "History and Genealogy of the Families of Old Fairfield", I, p. 660-2 (the attached source says volume III, but it is really volume I).  Thi
1 CONC s source can be found on the internet here:  https://dcms.lds.org/delivery/DeliveryManagerServlet?dps_pid=IE2130331.  According to this source, Richard Westcott settled in Wethersfield by 1636 and bought Samuel Clark's homestead; sold it to John St
1 CONC oddard, 1645, and removed to Fairfield, where he died before 11 March 1649/50.  He married Joanna, sister of the wife of Robert Sanford of Hartford.  She married (2) Nathaniel Baldwin of Fairfield, and (3) Thomas Skidmore of Fairfield.  Richard an
1 CONC d Joanna had children (1) John, b. about 1641; (2) Daniel, b. about 1643, (3) Joanna, b. about 1645 and married by 1665 John Weed of Stamford, and (4) Abigail, b. about 1647, married by 1669, Moses Knapp, of Stamford.  More information is given abo
1 CONC ut John and Daniel. 
1 CONT    
1 CONT In a book published by C.C. Baldwin about Nathaniel Baldwin's family, entitled "The Baldwin genealogy from 1500 to 1881," and which can be found in its entirely online and on ancestry.com, says on page 407 that "Mr. Savage, in his Genealogical Dict
1 CONC ionary, makes his widow [Nathaniel Baldwin's widow Joanna] marry Thomas Skidmore; but Mr. Byron A. Baldwin, a careful genealogist...states that she made a contract with Skidmore in regard to marriage, which was recorded, but did not marry him. Sh
1 CONC e married 3rd George Abbott, of Fairfield, and died in 1682."  However, Mr. C.C. Baldwin himself later corrected this error in his own supplement to his above work, entitled "The Baldwin genealogy supplement" and found online here: https://archive.
1 CONC org/details/baldwingenealogy1889bald.  See page 1082 where he writes that it "looks as if the Skidmore marriage came off after all." Actually, I think the evidence for the marriage to Skidmore is substantial.  by Lei Rachelle Child
0 @N3316@ NOTE Since Hartford Connecticut was not established until 1633 it is not possible that Joanna was born there before that time.
