0 @N3378@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Note: Died at age 73.
1 CONT This memorial is made as a Son of Edward and Magdalen (Oliver)
1 CONT Married Ellen (Newton) Adams married 1634 (Widow of John Adams)
1 CONT He was visiting his daughter in Salem, MA when he died and is buried there.
1 CONT (This is a cenotaph showing his name on the Monument, not his burial site)
1 CONT (Findagrave.com)
1 CONT Kenelm Winslow was the middle brother of five, who always seemed to be in the shadow of his older brothers. His oldest brother, Edward, traveled on the Mayflower in 1620. He played a strong, diplomatic role with the local Wampanoag tribes. He becam
1 CONC e close friends with the chief and served two terms as Governor of the colony. He wrote several books about the colony and traveled back and forth to London several times. 
1 CONT After the revolution in England, he also performed duties at the request of Oliver Cromwell. In fact, a statue of him exists today in his hometown of Droitwich, England. If that were not enough, Kenelm’s next oldest brother, John, became a very p
1 CONC rosperous businessman, eventually living in a mansion! By the time he died, at the age of 77, he was one of the wealthiest men in Boston. John had arrived in Plymouth in 1621, on the Fortune, the first ship to follow the Mayflower. This is importan
1 CONC t because the early colonists (known as old comers) received special privileges in terms of land grants that later settlers did not receive. John worked hard and took advantage of his early position to become an astounding success. And then there w
1 CONC as Kenelm. He arrived at least ten years after his older brothers, possibly more. He was first listed in Plymouth as a freeman in 1632. He had been delayed in part because he was living in London learning a trade. He was a joiner, which means he co
1 CONC uld make cabinets, coffins and other furniture by cleverly joining the wood without the use of any nails. This was obviously a useful trade that could provide him an adequate living in the colony, especially as there were not many other accomplishe
1 CONC d joiners in the early years. It appears that Kenelm did not have Edward’s diplomatic skills, nor John’s diligence. He made a decent living and provided for his family but never became wealthy. On several occasions his temper and lack of attent
1 CONC ion to his duties got him in trouble with the authorities. On at least one occasion he was fined 10 shillings for neglecting his duties as a surveyor for the highways. He also got into several civil disputes. In one instance he was sued by the fath
1 CONC er of a girl who had been working for him as a maid. Kenelm felt she owed him more work and so he kept some of her clothes and refused to give them back until she fulfilled her duties. He lost the case and was forced to return the clothes. On the s
1 CONC ame day, he was required to pay “sureties” for his good behavior after he made “opprobrious” remarks about the church. He apparently called some of the members “lyers”.   
1 CONT My own interpretation of these events is that members of the church testified against him during the court case concerning the maid and her clothes. He became angry, lost his temper and called them liars, getting himself into even bigger trouble. T
1 CONC his was not the first time Kenelm had lost his temper following a court case. A year before he had charged the court with acting improperly. Upon review by Myles Standish and six other men, it was determined that Kenelm was wrong. They ordered hi
1 CONC m to pay 10 pounds and threw him in jail. That time Kenelm wrote an apology and took back his complaint and got himself released from jail the same day. This time however he refused to pay the fine and was thrown in jail until the next court date.
1 CONT Kenelm had a few other run-ins with the court, mostly involving disputes over money, even including disputes with his younger brother. He also went to court to protect his daughter’s name which had been slandered by rumors and gossip. Despite the
1 CONC se court cases, and Kenelm’s temperamental outbursts, he appears to have been respected throughout the community. He held elected offices and served on several juries and committees. He lived in Plymouth, and later the outlying town of Marshfield
1 CONC , for forty years. Perhaps these skirmishes were not that significant when compared to the way he lived his life the rest of the time. Although Kenelm did not achieve the same level of success as his older brothers, he did build a nice home on a pi
1 CONC ece of property in Marshfield. The house and the property remained in the family for several generations before it was broken up and sold to pay debts. Incredibly, the house still stands in Marshfield and is still lived in today. Although we’d al
1 CONC ways like our ancestors to be heroes and larger than life, I like the fact that Kenelm wasn’t perfect. My impression of him was that he was a craftsman, who had a bit of an artistic temperament. He was most likely a perfectionist who had troubl
1 CONC e dealing with some of the realities of the people and situations around him.  Plymouth society was very controlling and required a great deal of conformity. I don’t think that it necessarily says anything too negative about Kenelm that he someti
1 CONC mes found himself at odds with these constraints. Kenelm had four children and lived nearly 400 years ago. Therefore, he has numerous descendants living today. 
1 CONT  I would be interested to hear from any of his descendants as to their interpretation of these events and their perspective on Kenelm’s character.
1 CONT (July 15, 2017 by Kraigthorne@yahoo.com)
1 CONT 
1 CONT From Wikipedia: In 1641, he moved to Marshfield, Mass. In 1653, Winslow became deputy in the Massachusetts General Court for eight years. Kenelm was a joiner, farmer, and shipper. In 1669, he was one of the 26 owners of Assonet, Mass.
0 @N3379@ NOTE The name "Grace" was very popular in Halifax in the early 1600s, and besides the marriage record between Grace Smith and Jonathan Fayrebanck in 20 May 1617, there was also a marriage record between a Grace Smith and a Michael Smith at the s
1 CONC ame church just a few months later, 15 September 1617.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT Just at the church of St. John the Baptist in Halifax, there are the following christening records which could belong to our Grace: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT December 1593, daughter of Samuel Smith
1 CONT June 1595, daughter of John Smith
1 CONT December 1595, daughter of Thomas Smith
1 CONT October 1599, daughter of Samuel Smith
1 CONT August 1600, daugher of Gibb Smith
1 CONT 
1 CONT If she married around age 20, really any of these baptismal records could belong to Grace.  If she married a little later in life, and that was the reason she stopped having children in 1629 (let's say she was 42 years old in 1629), then she coul
1 CONC d have been born as early as, say, 1589.
