0 @N3381@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT The book, Genealogy of the Fairbanks Family in America 1633-1897, published in 1897, identified the wife of Jonathan Fairbanks of Dedham as Grace Lee, giving no sources and no explanation.  Approximately 115 years later, Grace was identified throug
1 CONC h her marriage record as Grace SMITH, not Lee. See the article by Joseph, Ruth Fairbanks and Landberg, James Swan. "Jonathan Fairbank of Dedham, Massachusetts, and his family in the West Riding of Yorkshire, The New England Historical & Genealogica
1 CONC l Register, (New England Historic Genealogical Society, Boston, Mass.) July 2012, Vol. 166, Pages 165-187.  Also see original marriage record attached under Sources.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT Grace is not mentioned in any of her children's baptismal records.  The only records which we know for sure mention this Grace are her marriage record and her husband's will.  She has a likely death record in Dedham. Grace died either "28th 10th mo
1 CONC ., 1673", [the 10th month in those days was December] or "19:3:1676." One death record is for the wife of Jonathan, the other for her granddaughter, daughter of Jonathan Fairbanks and his wife Deborah. It is unclear which record is for which woman.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The name "Grace" was very popular in Halifax in the early 1600s, and besides the marriage record between Grace Smith and Jonathan Fayrebanck in 20 May 1617, there was also a marriage record between a Grace Smith and a Michael Smith at the same chur
1 CONC ch just a few months later, 15 September 1617.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT Just at the church of St. John the Baptist in Halifax, there are the following christening records which could belong to our Grace: 
1 CONT 
1 CONT December 1593, daughter of Samuel Smith
1 CONT June 1595, daughter of John Smith
1 CONT December 1595, daughter of Thomas Smith
1 CONT October 1599, daughter of Samuel Smith
1 CONT August 1600, daugher of Gibb Smith
1 CONT 
1 CONT If she married around age 20, really any of these baptismal records could belong to Grace.  If she married a little later in life, and that was the reason she stopped having children in 1629 (let's say she was 42 years old in 1629), then she coul
1 CONC d have been born as early as, say, 1589. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT St. John the Baptist church in Halifax dates back to about 1503, with parish registers dating back to 1538.  
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jonathan and Grace (Smith) Fairbank(s) had six known children, all b. at one of several towns or villages at or near Halifax, England. At the time, the Church of England required children to be baptized at the parish where the parents were then res
1 CONC iding:
1 CONT 
1 CONT • i. John Fairbanks, bapt. Feb. 8, 1617/8, d. Nov. 13, 1684; m. Mar. 16, 1641/2 at Dedham, Mass., Sarah Fiske. who d. Nov. 26, 1683 at Dedham. Eight children of the family.
1 CONT 
1 CONT * ii. George Fairbanks, bapt. Nov 28, 1619 at Shelf near Halifax, d. Jan. 10, 1682/3 at Medfield, Mass.; m. Oct. 26, 1646 at Dedham, Mary Adams, who d. Aug. 11, 1711.
1 CONT 
1 CONT • iii. Mary Fairbanks, bapt. in Feb. 1621/2 at Shelf near Halifax, d. June 4, 1684 at Dedham, Mass.; m. 1) Apr. 2, 1644 at Dedham, Mass., Michael Metcalf, Jr., b. or bapt. Aug. 29, 1620 at Norwich, England. Five children of the family. She m. 2
1 CONC ) Aug. 2, 1654 at Dedham, Christopher Smith. They had one known child.
1 CONT 
1 CONT • iv. Jonas Fairbanks, bapt. Mar. 6, 1624/5, was killed in a massacre by the Indians Feb. 10, 1675/6 at Lancaster, Mass.; m. May 28, 1658 at Lancaster, Mass., Lydia Prescott where seven children are of record.
1 CONT 
1 CONT • v. Susan Fairbanks, bapt. Dec. 10, 1627 at Thorton-in-Craven near Halifax, d. July 8, 1659 at Dedham, Mass.; m. Oct. 12, 1647 at Dedham as his 1st wife, Sgt. Ralph Day. Five children of the family. Ralph m. 2) Nov. 15, 1659 at Dedham, Abigail C
1 CONC rafts, widow of John Ruggles, dau. of Lieut. Griffin and Alice Crafts of Roxbury, Mass.
1 CONT 
1 CONT • vi. Jonathan Fairbanks, Jr., b. circa 1630, d. Jan. 28, 1711/2 at Dedham, Mass.; m. Oct. 4, 1649 at Cambridge, Mass., Deborah Shepard, dau. of Edward Shepard and Violet Charnould, who d. Sept. 7, 1705 at Dedham. Eight children of the family.
0 @N3382@ NOTE Quoting explanation:
1 CONT In 1638, Joseph Kingsbury provided a portion of his land at the south end of his lot to enlarge the accommodation for the dead. The boundaries of the burial place continued the same for about 175 years, an area of about one acre. For a period of on
1 CONC e hundred years, this was the only place of burial in the town of Dedham, and some parts were dug over several times to furnish graves for successive generations. Where a stone was not setup, the place of interment was soon forgotten, and so receiv
1 CONC ed another occupant. From the nature of things several thousand have here found their last resting place. One after another, for nearly two hundred years as the ashes were gathered, with no monument to distinquish one from another, it is truly fitt
1 CONC ing that the most beautiful trees of the forest should weave a canopy over their common resting place. [Excerpted from "Epitaphs in the Old Burial Place," Dedham, Mass., by Rev. Carlos Slafter, Dedham Historical Society (1888).]
