0 @N3407@ NOTE Jonathan Fairbanks (1594 – December 5, 1668) was an English colonist born in Heptonstall, Halifax, in the West Riding of Yorkshire, England who immigrated to New England in 1633. Around 1636 Fairbanks built the Fairbanks House in Dedham
1 CONC , Massachusetts which is today the oldest surviving wood framed house in North America.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Family
1 CONT Fairbanks married Grace Smith in Halifax on May 20, 1617.[1] Together they had six children.[1] To celebrate the Fairbanks' 400th wedding anniversary, the Fairbanks Family in America offered half price admission to the house, as well as wedding cak
1 CONC e and popcorn on May 20, 2017.[1]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Settlement in New England
1 CONT Jonathan Fairbanks arrived in Boston, Massachusetts Bay Colony, with his family in 1633. The Fairbanks family remained in Boston about three years, before settling in Dedham, as one of the earliest settler families. The family purchased 12 acres o
1 CONC f land, and in 1637 master carpenters began constructing their home.[1] Jonathan Fairbanks signed the Covenant when the town was founded and named.[2]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Conversion to Christianity
1 CONT 
1 CONT Old Village Cemetery, the final resting place of Fairbanks
1 CONT Jonathan Fairbanks had "long stood off from the church upon some scruples about public profession of faith and the covenant, yet after divers loving conferences..., [In 1646] he made such a declaration of his faith and conversion to God and profess
1 CONC ion of subjection to the ordinances of Christ in the church that he was readily and gladly received by the whole church."[3] Fairbanks became a member of the First Church in Dedham, which espoused a Reformed theology (Calvinist) in the seventeent
1 CONC h century.[4]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Jonathan Fairbanks died in Dedham, December 5, 1668. Grace Fairbanks died 28th 10 mo. 1673, or 19: 3: 1676.[2] Fairbanks was buried in the Old Village Cemetery.[5]
1 CONT 
1 CONT As was common at the time Jonathan used several spellings of his surname: Fairbanke, Fairebanke, Fayerbanke, and on his will Fairbanck.[6] His sons and grandsons began spelling the name Fairbank or Fairbanks. The spelling Fairbanks carried on for 1
1 CONC 5 generations.
