0 @N3446@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Do not merge with other William French's.  Recent scholarship has now proven his parents, despite the lack of a christening record. Please refer to the 2023 article in the New England Historical and Genealogical Register [NEHGR], vol. 177, p. 31-4
1 CONC 4 for details. 
1 CONT 
1 CONT The above article superceedes William French's profile (below) in the Great Migration series, and the following profile:
1 CONT http://www.frenchfamilyassoc.com/FFA/CHARTSWEB/ChartE002.htm
1 CONT ===
1 CONT Robert Charles Anderson, George F. Sanborn, and Melinde Lutz Sanborn, "The Great Migration: Immigrants to New England 1634-1635," Volume II, C-F (Boston, MA: New England Historic Genealogical Society, 2001), 588-593.
1 CONT 
1 CONT WILLIAM FRENCH
1 CONT 
1 CONT ORIGIN: Unknown: Now known to be Bures St. Mary. 
1 CONT MIGRATION: 1635 on the Defence
1 CONT FIRST RESIDENCE: Cambridge
1 CONT REMOVES: Billerica 1652
1 CONT 
1 CONT OCCUPATION: Tailor [MLR 1:205-7, 3:38-39].
1 CONT 
1 CONT CHURCH MEMBERSHIP: Admission to Cambridge church prior to 3 March 1635/6 implied by freemanship. In the January 1658/9 list of Cambridge church members are "William French & Elizabeth his wife both members in full communion, Their children[:] Eliza
1 CONC beth now Elliot & now joined at Dedham. Mary baptized in England between 2 and 3 years old at her father's joining. John baptized by Mr. Hooker in Cambridge. Sarah, Jacob, Hannah born and baptized in this Church" [CaChR 7-8].
1 CONT 
1 CONT FREEMAN: 3 March 1635/6 (seventh in a sequence of eleven Cambridge men) [MBCR 1:371].
1 CONT 
1 CONT EDUCATION: William French sent to a friend in England "the relation of one Indian of two years profession, that I took from his own mouth by an interpreter, because he cannot speak or understand one word of English"; this document was then include
1 CONC d in a pamphlet, Strength out of Weakness; or a Glorious Manifestation of the Further Progresse of the Gospel among the Indians of New-England. Held Forth in Sundry Letter from divers Ministers and others...," published in London in 1652 [MHSC 3:4:
1 CONC 192-93]. Signed his will.
1 CONT 
1 CONT OFFICES: Deputy from Billerica to Massachusetts Bay General Court, 19 December 1660, 27 May 1663, 3 August 1664 [MBCR 4:1:449, 4:2:72, 117]
1 CONT 
1 CONT Empowered to marry others in Billerica and Chelmsford, 18 October 1659, 15 May 1667 (Billerica only) [MBCR 4:2:336]. Petit jury, 3 December 1639 [MBCR 1:283].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Admitted to Ancient and Honorable Artillery Company, 1638 [HAHAC 1:60].
1 CONT 
1 CONT Sergeant, 27 September 1642 [MBCR 2:29-30]. Lieutenant, nominated 1 October 1645 [MBCR 2:137] and confirmed 26 May 1647 [MBCR 2:187]. His inventory included an "old musket & gun barrel" valued at 12s.
1 CONT 
1 CONT ESTATE: In the 8 February 1635/6 Cambridge list of "those men who have houses in the town," "Will[iam] French" had one [CaTR 18]. On 6 February 1636/7, William French was granted two acres of land [CaTR 26]. In 1639 William French held two parcel
1 CONC s of land: "bought of Mr. Richard Champnes, one dwelling house & garden"; and "given by the town two acres of land in the New West Field" [CaBOP 66]. By 1639 William French had sold to John Sill "one house with garden and backside in the town" [CaB
1 CONC OP 57].
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the Cambridge land inventory of 6 September 1642, William French held four parcels: "in the town one dwelling house with about half a rood of ground"; "in the New West Field two acres"; "on the south side of Charles River, eight acres more or le
1 CONC ss being the seventh lot in the lower division of lots"; and "in the upper division of those lots eight acres more or less being the three and fiftieth lot there" [CaBOP 100, 331]
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1645 William French was granted "six acres & half" "on the west side of Monotamye River" [CaBOP 129]; on or soon after 10 December 1646, William French sold to Edward Winship "in the lots beyond Menotomy six acres & half" [CaBOP 132].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 15 August 1646, William French sold to Andrew Stevenson "four acres of land more or less in New West Field" [CaBOP 130]. On an unknown date, William French held five acres in Fresh Pond Meadow [CaBOP 333].
1 CONT 
1 CONT In the division of "Shaw Shine" on 4 June 1652, "Lt. Will[ia]m French" received Lot #24, 150 acres [CaTR 97].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 27 February 1654[/5], William French of Cambridge, tailor, sold to Gilbert Crackbone of Cambridge, husbandman, "four acres of land...in a planting field commonly called there West Field" [MLR 3:38-39].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 10 June 1656, "William French of Cambridge...,tailor," sold to "William Barret of the same place, tailor,...my now mansion house situated in Cambridge before named with about half a rood of land adjoining to the same...,also a barn and outhous
1 CONC e standing on the east side the highway with about half a rood of land adjoining thereto" [MLR 1:205-7].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On [blank] February 1667[/8?], "William French of Billerica...in consideration of a valuable sum of money, to me well and truly paid, by Sgt. John Parker (now deceased) sometimes of Billerica aforesaid, for the use and behoof of Jonathan Danforth
1 CONC " of Billerica, deeded to Jonathan Danforth a parcel of meadow in Billerica "containing by estimation one acre and three quarters" [MLR 4:255-57].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 29 November 1678, "William French of Billerica...for and in consideration of the love & affection he beareth to his son Jacob French, as also for the full & final accomplishment of a contract & agreement made between the said W[illia]m Frenc
1 CONC h & Elder Richard Champney of Cambridge deceased, upon the marriage of the said Jacob French to Mary, the daughter of the said Elder Chamney," deeded to Jacob French a parcel of land in Billerica "containing by estimation twenty-five acres lying o
1 CONC n the Pine Plain" [MLR 7:59].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 21 December 1680, "Mary French administrator to the estate of her deceased husband John Sternes," along with "John Stearnes" and "Samuel Starnes" petitioned for a division of the estate of John Stearns. To this petition were appended two other i
1 CONC tems: "Request of selectmen of Billerica for the Court to take into consideration the condition of the family of one Lt. W[illia]m French of Billerica is by God's hand through impotency & weakness unfit to govern his domestic concerns, at the reque
1 CONC st of his friend Deacon Tompson & his son Jacob French are empowered to assist his wife in the ordering & disposing of his estate, so as may best conduce for the supply of the family," 21 December 1680 [Scott Gen 196, location of petition not state
1 CONC d].
1 CONT 
1 CONT In his will, dated 5 June 1679 and proved 20 December 1681, "W[illia]m French of Billerica, aged about seventy & six years," declared that "I have already given to all my children that have been already married their portions, I only add to them a
1 CONC s followeth: to the eldest son of John French, to W[illia]m the son of Jacob French, to Elizabeth daughter [of] Richard Ellis, to Jonathan the son of Jonathan Hides, to the eldest daughter of Jonathan Peake, to Marah the daughter of John Brackett
1 CONC , which are all my grandchildren, to each person as aforesaid twenty shillings"; residue to "my beloved wife, & to those children born to me by her, to be divided to each at the discretion of our honored county court after my decease"; "my belove
1 CONC d wife & my son Jacob French" executors; at the time of probate, there was "allowed to the [illegible] of the children £6 13s. 4d., to the widow 1.3 the remainder, to the widow the remainder 2/3 equally to be let out by Lt. Jonath[an] Danforth, Jo
1 CONC seph Thompson & Josiah Convarse to each his part & the widow to have the benefit of the children's portion for the bringing up & education until they come of age to choose their guardians'" [MPR 5:250-51].
1 CONT 
1 CONT The inventory of the estate of "Lt. W[illia]m French of Billerica who deceased 20 November 1681 being his 78 year of his age," taken 6 December 1681, totalled £231 12s. 10d. (against which were debts of about £25), of which £104 was real estate
1 CONC : "house & barn, homestead, meadows & outlands," £104; presented 8 December 1681 by "Mary French executrix to the estate of Lt. French her deceased husband" [MPR 5:251-53].
1 CONT 
1 CONT On 6 December 1687 "the estate of Lt. William French of Billerica (who deceased the 20.9m.1681)" was divided, "to the widow one-third part of the whole estate and to the three children the remaining two-thirds, equally," the widow receiving £60 14
1 CONC s. 10d., "Mary French (now Sharp)," £40 10s., "Sarah French," £40 10s., and "Hannah French," £40 10s.; this division was acknowledged on 20 August 1722 by "Sarah Crosbey, Mary Dunklin & Hannah Child (daughters of the within mentioned Will[ia]m F
1 CONC rench deceased)" [MLR 16:413-14; MPR Case #8528].
1 CONT 
1 CONT BIRTH: About 1604 (aged 30 in 1635 [Hotten 100]; "aged about seventy & six years" on 5 June 1679 [MPR 5:250-51]; d. 20 November 1681 "in 78th year").
1 CONT 
1 CONT DEATH: Billerica 20 November 1681 "in 78th year."
1 CONT 
1 CONT MARRIAGE: (1) By about 1625 Elizabeth ____ (assuming she was his first wife, and that Francis was a child of this couple). She died at Billerica 31 March 1668. (2) Billerica 6 May 1669 Mary (Lothrop) Stearns, widow of John Stearns. She married (3
1 CONC ) Billerica 29 June 1687 Isaac Mixer [Bailey 3:11].
1 CONT 
1 CONT BIBLIOGRAPHIC NOTE: In 1911 Elizabeth French published information from English records which demonstrated that the William French baptized at Halstead, Essex, on 15 March 1603 could not have been the immigrant to New England [NEHGR 65:284-86].
0 @N3447@ NOTE States when he and his family arrived, on what ship, the name of his wife, children and a biography.
0 @N3448@ NOTE 6. THOMAS, born in England, and probably in Egerton, about 1621, as we infer from his own deposition, taken April 4, 1701, in which he states that he is "about 80 years of age." The will of his father designates him as his eldest son, whic
1 CONC h is proof that the John who was baptized in 1617-18 was not living. The probability is that his birth succeeded the withdrawal of his father from the curacy of the parish church in Egerton, Kent, where the older children were recorded. At least th
1 CONC is is certain, that the baptism of his older sister is the last baptism at Egerton found on the copy of the baptisms which the father made.
1 CONT 
1 CONT At the age of about thirteen he came with his father to Scituate, at which place we find this first. record regarding him in this country: " My sonn Thomas Lothropp joined May 4, 1637. "
1 CONT 
1 CONT This was his admission to the church in Scituate, from which he removed with his father to Barnstable, in 1639, where he soon gained distinction among the pioneers of the new town.
1 CONT 
1 CONT The second record we find is in Barnstable, as follows: "My sonn Thomas and brother Larnett's daughter, widow Ewer, were married in the Bay (Boston) Dec. 11, 1639." The "daughter" above referred to, was Sarah, daughter of William Larned, and wido
1 CONC w of Thomas Ewer.
1 CONT 
1 CONT Elizabeth, a daughter of this Sarah Ewer by her former husband, was baptized April 9, 1641, and was married, as we learn from a third record from the same hand as the above, to Thomas Blossom, June 18, 1645, "at my sonn Thomas his house," the Thoma
1 CONC s Blossom above having been born in Leyden about 1620.
1 CONT 
1 CONT In 1641 Thomas Lothrop is reported as land surveyor at Barnstable, and in 1643 as one liable to bear arms.
1 CONT 
1 CONT He became quite a large landholder and an enterprising business man. He was enrolled as freeman June 3, 1656. He served the town in several offices, indicating his standing as in honor among his fellow townsmen. His death took place in 1707.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 6. THOMAS. Charlestown, Mass.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 16. Mary, born Oct. 4, 1640, and married as his second wife, in Barnstable, Dec, 1656, John Stearns of Billerica, Mass. He died March 5, 1668-9, and she married May 6, 1669, Capt. William French of Billerica. Capt. French died Nov. 20, 1681, aged 7
1 CONC 8. She married for her third husband, in 1684, Isaac Mixer, Jr., of Watertown, and she was his third wife. She was living in 1735 "very aged." John Stearns was the eldest son and third child of Isaac and Mary Stearns of Watertown. This Isaac came t
1 CONC o America with his wife and three children, as Bond supposes, in the same ship with Gov. Winthrop in 1630, and settled in Watertown where live more children were added to his family, and where he died in 1670. The children of Mary Lothrop, by her f
1 CONC irst husband, were: Isaac Stearns, born April 17, 1658, and died Oct. 9. 1659; Samuel Stearns, born Sept. 3, 1659; Isaac Stearns, born Dec. 31, 1661; Nathaniel Stearns, born Nov. 30, 1663; Thomas Stearns, born Dec. 6, 1665. Her children by her seco
1 CONC nd husband were: Mary French, born April 30. 1670, who married Nathaniel Dunkler of Watertown and had three children, Hezekiah, Nathaniel, and Damans ; Sarah French, born Oct. 29, 1571, and married Joseph Crosby of Billerica. and had ten children
1 CONC ; David, William, Thomas, Robert, Sarah, Rachel, Mary, Prudence, Deborah, and Hannah; Abigail French, born April 14, 1673, and died April 13, 1674; Hannah French, born 1676, and married John Child.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 17. Hannah, baptized in Barnstable, Oct. 18, 1642.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 18. Thomas, baptized July 7, 1644, and was living in 1697.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 19. Meletiah, baptized Nov. 22, 1646, married May 20, 1667, Sarah Farrar, daughter of Thomas and Elizabeth (Hood) Farrar of Lynn, Mass. He died Feb. 6, 1712, and his widow, May 23, 1712, aged 64 years. His will, dated Oct. 29, 1711. and probated Fe
1 CONC b. 25, 1711-12. names as legatees his wife Sarah, "his" grandson Melatiah, son of my son Isaac; granddaughter of Jedidiah, "my five children, Isaac, Shubal, Tabitha, Dimock, Elizabeth Lothrop," and Sarah Troop and sons Joseph and Thomas.
1 CONT 
1 CONT 20. Bethiah, born July 23, 1649, and married in July. 1668, John Hinckley, probably a son of Samuel Hinckley. They settled in Barnstable where she died, July 10, 1694, and he, Dec. 7, 1709. Their children were: Sarah Hinckley, born in the end of Ma
1 CONC y. 1669, and married John Crocker; Samuel Hinckley, born Feb. 2, 1670-1. He settled in Stonington, Ct., and was living in 1710; Bethia Hinckley, born latter end of March, 1673, and died April 12, 1715; Hannah Hinckley, born middle of May, 1675, an
1 CONC d married June 2, 1708, Benjamin Lewis; Jonathan Hinckley, born Feb. 15. 1677; Ichabod Hinckley, born Aug. 28, 1680; Gershom Hinckley, born April 2, 1682. In the settlement of the estate and in the mother's will, the following children are also nam
1 CONC ed : Mary Hinckley; Abigail Hinckley; Mercy Hinckley, who in her will names also a brother, Job Hinckley.
