0 @N3453@ NOTE The record provides the evidence to disprove that William French baptized 15 March, son of Thomas and Anne French is the William French who immigrated to America. Instead, the William French who was baptized 15 March 1603 and was the son o
1 CONC f Thomas and Anne French, died in London in 1621, unmarried.
0 @N3454@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Melathiah (aka Milcah) Kelway was born in 1619 in Essex, England. Her father was Walter Kelway of Chelmsford, Essex. Melethiah Kelway ... She passed away in 1669 [sic=date unknown; AFTER April 28, 1669] in Boston, Suffolk, Massachusetts. [1]
1 CONT Melatiah Kelway married Thomas Snow in 1637 in Chelmsford, Essex, England, when she was 19 and he was 36 years old. Shortly thereafter they emigrated together to Boston, Massachusetts Bay Colony, where their first child (Melatiah Snow) was born o
1 CONC n September 30, 1638.
1 CONT Thomas Snow's Will was proven on April 28, 1669; in it he names his wife Milcah (aka Melatiah) Snow as executor/administrator for his children. This indicates that she survived him and died sometime after April 1669.
1 CONT Boston land records reveal that on December 16, 1667, Milcah Snow mortgaged her house in Boston, "in which was fixed the sign of the Dove". In 1672, [Thomas Snow's relict / widow] sold a pasture. She died intestate shortly before August 2, 1678, wh
1 CONC en her estate was divided among her [four living children] heirs who were, Samuel, Mehetabel, Meletiah, wife of Samuel Fisher; and Abigail, wife of William Wright, Jr.[2]
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT     1. First-hand information as remembered by Doug Irwin, Tuesday, November 11, 2014. Replace this citation if there is another source.
1 CONT     2. Families from MD, VA, DC as well as VT, MA, NY - Thomas Snow
1 CONT 
1 CONT 
1 CONT --------------------------
1 CONT 
1 CONT Wanted to add this source as well:
1 CONT 
1 CONT Find a Grave, database and images (https://www.findagrave.com : accessed 03 April 2021), memorial page for Milcah Kelway Snow Wright (unknown–1677), Find a Grave Memorial no. 37181153, ; Maintained by Linda Mac (contributor 47062703) Unknown.
1 CONT 
1 CONT "Thomas Snow married by 1638 Milcah Kelway, daughter of Walter Kelway of Chelmsford, Essex.
1 CONT They had four children: Melatiah, Hannah, Samuel, & Mehitable.
1 CONT She married (2) by 1672 William Wright.
1 CONT In his will of 1 October 1650, "Walter Kelway of Chelmsford, Essex," included a bequest to "my three daughters in New England, namely my daughter Margaret Mountague, my daughter Melcas Snow and my daughter Mary Lane"; William S. Appleton identifie
1 CONC d these three women as the wives of Griffin Mountague, Thomas Snow and William Lane.
1 CONT The given name Milcah appears twice in the Bible, as daughter of Haran and as daughter of Zelophehad. There is one Melatiah in the Bible, clearly a man, which may explain the Boston birth record calling this Snow child a son. Search in the electron
1 CONC ic version of Torrey's index to seventeenth-century marriages in New England reveals twelve occurences of the given name Melatiah or Meletiah, four men and eight women. Melatiah Snow is the earliest by nearly a decade, and eight of the twelve wer
1 CONC e born in the 1660s or 1670s.
1 CONT Secondary sources include a second daughter Hannah, twin sister of Abigail: "Hannah (twin), b. 10 Mar. 1651-2, probably d. before 14 Mar. 1651-2, since there is no record of baptism." This is a possible interpretation of the original records, but w
1 CONC e suggest an alternative explanation. The birth records for Hannah and Abigail occur on the same page of the published Boston vital records, but with ten other births intervening. Interestingly, two of the births intervening are for Joseph and Mary
1 CONC , children of JOHN MYLAM {1635, Boston}; these two entries are adjacent to one another, which is what one would expect for twins. We conclude that the 10 March 1651/2 birth record for a Hannah Snow is a spurious duplicate of the correct record fo
1 CONC r Abigail Snow."
