0 @N3551@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT      Robert Lockwood  (1600-1658) & Susannah Norman (1612-1660) 
1 CONT  immigrant
1 CONT Robert (Edmund, Richard, Thomas,Robert) was born January 14, 1599, Combs, England; and died before September, 11, 1658, Fairfield, Connecticut. 
1 CONT He married 1) 1634 Mrs. Robert Lockwood  (2 ) Susannah Norman in 1643 in Watertown, Massachusetts. She was born in 1616 in Dorcester, England;died December 23, in Stamford, or Greenwich, Connecticut. Susannah was the daughter of Capt. Richard Norma
1 CONC n and came to America with her father,settling in Cape Ann, Massachusetts in 1636. Robert married ( 3 ) Susannah Sension abt. 1656. Elinor Lockwood, born October, 1609, Bures, St. Mary's, Suffolk Co.,England; died August 16, 1658 in Stamford, Conne
1 CONC cticut. She married Nicholas Knapp, 1630, Watertown, Massachusetts. Nicholas was born May
1 CONT         "An emigrant about 1630 from England to New England, a settler at Watertown,where he was admitted a freeman 9 March 1636/7, and a resident in 1646 of Fairfield, Connecticut., where he was admitted a freeman 20 May 1652 and died in 1658,
1 CONT whose wife, Susannah Norman married secondly Jeffrey Ferris and died at Greenwich, Conn., 23 December 1660, ..."
1 CONT 
1 CONT Citation Information: "The Great Migration Begins: Immigrants to New England 1620-1633," Volumes I-III, pp. 334-36:
1 CONT   "Richard Norman  
1 CONT   Origin: Unknown 
1 CONT   Migration: 1626 
1 CONT   First Residence: Salem
1 CONT   Removes: Marblehead
1 CONT   Occupation: Fisherman or shipwright.
1 CONT   Education: Signed deed of 1653 by mark 3:369]. 
1 CONT   Offices: Coroner's jury on body of William Bateman, 18 September 1630 [MBCR 1:78]. On 18 May 1631 "Rich[ard] Norman is fined 2s. 6d. for his negligence in watching" [MBCR 1:87]. 
1 CONT   Estate: In Salem land grant of 1636 received twenty acres [STR 1:19, 24]. In the 25 December 1637 division of marsh and meadow, granted three-quarters of an acre, with a household of four [STR 1:103]. 
1 CONT      Granted twenty acres "that was Mr. Thorndeck's," 29 January 1637/8 [STR 1:65]. A grant of 20 January 1640/1 to Allen Converse refers to "that lot which was Richard Norman's near the head of the river," and adjoining Mr. Endicott's farm [STR 1:
1 CONC 109]. 
1 CONT      In his will, dated 14 October 1672, son-in-law Robert Morgan of Salem mentions the twelve acres of land in Manchester "which my wife's father Norman gave unto her in the great plain" [EPR 2:355]. 
1 CONT      On 22 April 1653 Richard Norman the elder deeded to his son Richard Norman his house and ten acre lot "in Marvellheade upon Darbe Fort side," along with cow commons [EQC 3:369]. 
1 CONT   Birth: By about 1587 based on estimated date of marriage. 
1 CONT   Death: After 22 April 1653 [EQC 3:369], but before 27 June 1664 [EQC 3:369, when Thomas Millett Sr. acknowledged "old Richard Norman's act and deed"]. 
1 CONT   Marriage: By about 1612 ____ ____; on 9 July 1645 the "wife of Richard Norman Sr." testified in court [EQC 1:82]; no further record. (Two women by the name of Arabella Norman were admitted to Salem church, one in May 1637 and the other on 25 Febr
1 CONC uary 1637/8 [SChR 6, 7]. One of these is certainly the wife of John Norman, son of Richard. The second is probably a clerical error, but just might be the wife of Richard Sr.) 
1 CONT   Children: 
1 CONT       i JOHN, b. say 1612; m. before May 1637 Arabella ____ [SChR 6; EQC 1:74 (when he certifies the births of children with wife Arabella)]. 
1 CONT       ii MARGARET, b. say 1613; m. (1) by about 1633 Robert Morgan (who mentions "my wife's father Norman" in his will [EPR 2:355]; their eldest Child, Samuel, m. in 1658). (Margaret is said to have married second, after 1673, Samuel Fowler, but th
1 CONC e evidence for this is not seen [Hoyt 162].) 
1 CONT       iii SUSANNA, b. say 1615; m. by 1635 Robert Lockwood (who refused a Salem houselot grant "beyond his father Norman's" [STR 1:47]). 
1 CONT       iv FLORENCE, b. say 1619; m. (1) by about 1639 John Hart (daughter Elizabeth m. in 1659; husband of daughter Florence called son-in-law of Thomas Whittridge in 1672 [Sarah Stone Anc 38]); m. (2) about 1657 Thomas Whittridge (who called Richar
1 CONC d Norman his wife's brother and with whom she had three sons after 1657 [EPR 2:287; Sarah Stone Anc 46-47]). 
1 CONT       v RICHARD, b. about 1623 (deposed aged about fifty 19 March 1673/4 [EQC 5:256], deposed aged about fifty-seven at June Term 1680 [EQC 7:386]); m. by about 1650 Margaret ____ (daughter Elizabeth "d. in 1732, aged eighty-two" [Sarah Stone Anc 5
1 CONC 2]). 
1 CONT   Comments: In his deposition of 20 January 1680/1 Richard Brackenbury included "old Goodman Norman and his son" among those who were already at Salem when Endicott arrived in 1628 [EIHC 13:138]. 
1 CONT      On 17 September 1640 "Erasmus James and Richard Norman, sr., [were] presented for defective fences on Darby fort side. Not proved" [EQC 1:194]. 
1 CONT      Richard Norman was evidently in a fishing or shipbuilding business with his son, since "Rich[ard] Norman, Jno. Norman and Company" were sued by John Devereux at July Term 1642 and again at December Term 1642 [EQC 1:42, 45]. The conflict grew h
1 CONC eated and Devereux further sued Richard Norman for defamation at December Term 1642 [EQC 1:45]. 
1 CONT      At July Term 1642 Norman witnessed against Matthew Gillet who stole a piece of soap from John Norman "to wash his shirts with, as he said" [EQC 1:44]. When his son Richard, Jr., was fined for "slighting ordinances and carrying a burden on Lord
1 CONC 's day," Richard Sr. answered for him in court, February 1642/3 [EQC 1:51]. It is likely, as Davis says, that "Norman was probably not of the Puritan persuasion" [Sarah Stone Anc 45]. 
1 CONT      Pope incorrectly applied the probate of the younger Richard to the elder. Savage thought that there were two John Normans, a brother of Richard the immigrant, and a son, but the records all apply to one man, son of Richard. 
1 CONT   Bibliographic Note: In 1930 Walter Goodwin Davis prepared one of his typical eloquent but sparsely referenced treatments of this family [Sarah Stone Anc 45-54]."
