0 @N3734@ NOTE === Life Sketch ===
1 CONT Our American Ancestor, Edward Haraden, was born before 1627 an undoubtedly in England. He may have brought his wife Sarah from England with him. He died intestate at Gloucester, Mass. 17 May 1683. His widow Sarah died 4 March 1691, also at Gloucest
1 CONC er. Aged about 69 years.
1 CONT Edward Haraden's story begins about 1650 at Ipswich, Mass. On 23 Sept 1652, he got a writ of attachment there against the ground and garden of Humphfrey Griffin in Ipswich for debt; and on 14 January 1655 he acquired a house and about 30 acres of l
1 CONC and from Thomas Knowlton, a shoemaker in Ipswich, previously  owned by his deceased brother William Knowlton. This land, for which Haraden was to pay 34 lbs. in cattle and wheat,joined on the west land Haraden had previously owned, was bounded on t
1 CONC he north by the Chebago River and also lay against Haraden's Creek which flowed into the river near an island.
1 CONT Also in 1678, while living in Gloucester, he deposed about a certain row of boundary trees in Ipswich which had been standing since before 1652.
1 CONT  He moved from Ipswich to Gloucester about April 1657 and that year bought the entire property of Robert Dutch of Gloucester. This purchase included a tract of land on Planter's Neck where Dutch had a fishing stage but the place of Haraden's reside
1 CONC nce and business probably was at the head of Lobster Cove where he lived the rest of his life.  He apparently was the first , or among the first, permanent settler in that part of the town.
1 CONT He may of have had financial problems. Dutch threatened him with a suit for debt in March 1662 but later withdrew it.
1 CONT During King Philips war, either Edward or his son Edward served in Captain Joseph's Gardner's company and had more than tow pounds of wages due him for services on 29 Feb 1676.  Edward Sr.  served on a trial jury in Gloucester in 1678. 
1 CONT Ferris suggests the probability that " an epedemic must have been raging in 1683 when he died) poor widow Sarah lost 3 members of her family between March 4 and May 17 in 1683, her son Andrew at the age of twenty-two, son Thomas at the age of eight
1 CONC een and finally her husband at the age of fifty-six. He left an estate inventoried at about 285 lbs. and she was appointed administratrix. Though Edward had been called a fisherman, the fact that he left 9 cows, 3 oxen, two young bullocks, two year
1 CONC lings, five calves and other live stock, argues that he was occupied in agriculture.
1 CONT James Stevens who had quarreled bitterly with Edward Haraden in 1666-67 about ownership of a hay meadow, but whom whose differences must of been solved satisfactorily, was called upon to inventory the estate of both Edward Sr. and his son Andrew.
1 CONT Edward and Sarah had four children before they reached Gloucester, Mass.The first one Edward may have been born in England, although it is more likely that all four were born in Ipswich, Mass. Beginning with Andrew the last eight were recorded in G
1 CONC loucester.
